answer:
Boxing wraps a value type in a heap-allocated object so it can be treated as `object` or an interface; unboxing copies the value back out, with a runtime type check. Each box is an allocation, so boxing in a hot loop creates garbage collection pressure rather than just costing a few instructions.

```csharp
int n = 42;
object boxed = n;        // allocation on the heap, value copied in
int back = (int)boxed;   // type check, then copy out

// The costly version: one box per element, plus a cast on every read.
ArrayList list = new();
for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) list.Add(i);   // 1,000,000 allocations

// The fix: a generic collection stores int inline, no boxing at all.
List<int> typed = new(1_000_000);
for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) typed.Add(i);
```

Because the box is a copy, mutating a boxed value type does not affect the original, which is a classic source of silent bugs with mutable structs.

**Logic**

An interface is a contract: it says what a type can do, carries no state, and a class may implement many. An abstract class is a partial implementation: it can hold fields, constructors and shared logic, and a class may inherit only one.

The rule of thumb is "can-do" versus "is-a". A type can be comparable, disposable and serialisable at once, so those are interfaces. A type is a specific kind of payment method sharing real behaviour with its siblings, which is an abstract base. Since C# 8 interfaces may carry default implementations, which narrows the gap but does not close it: interfaces still cannot hold instance state.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

// Capability: many unrelated types can be auditable.
public interface IAuditable
{
    string AuditLine();

    // C# 8+: a default implementation, so implementers may opt out of writing it.
    void WriteAudit() => Console.WriteLine($"[audit] {AuditLine()}");
}

// Identity: these ARE payment methods and share real behaviour and state.
public abstract class PaymentMethod
{
    protected PaymentMethod(string owner) => Owner = owner;   // state + constructor

    public string Owner { get; }

    public abstract decimal Fee(decimal amount);              // each must decide

    // Shared template: the algorithm lives once, the varying step is deferred.
    public decimal Charge(decimal amount)
    {
        decimal total = amount + Fee(amount);
        Console.WriteLine($"{Owner} charged {total:F2} via {GetType().Name}");
        return total;
    }
}

public class CardPayment : PaymentMethod, IAuditable
{
    public CardPayment(string owner) : base(owner) { }
    public override decimal Fee(decimal amount) => amount * 0.02m;
    public string AuditLine() => $"card payment for {Owner}";
}

public class BankTransfer : PaymentMethod
{
    public BankTransfer(string owner) : base(owner) { }
    public override decimal Fee(decimal amount) => 0.50m;
}

// Unrelated to payments, yet still auditable: that is why it is an interface.
public class UserLogin : IAuditable
{
    public string AuditLine() => "user signed in";
}

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var methods = new List<PaymentMethod> { new CardPayment("Priya"), new BankTransfer("Sam") };
        foreach (var m in methods) m.Charge(100m);

        // One interface, two entirely unrelated implementers.
        var auditable = new List<IAuditable> { new CardPayment("Priya"), new UserLogin() };
        foreach (var a in auditable) a.WriteAudit();
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
Priya charged 102.00 via CardPayment
Sam charged 100.50 via BankTransfer
[audit] card payment for Priya
[audit] user signed in
```

**Logic**

Encapsulation hides internal state behind a controlled surface, so invariants cannot be broken from outside. Abstraction exposes what a type does while hiding how. Inheritance shares behaviour between related types. Polymorphism lets one call site invoke different behaviour depending on the runtime type.

The pillar most often described badly is encapsulation: it is not "make fields private and add a getter and setter for each", which exposes exactly as much as a public field did. It means the type owns its invariants.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

// ENCAPSULATION: the balance cannot go negative, and no caller can force it to.
public class Account
{
    private decimal _balance;

    public Account(decimal opening)
    {
        if (opening < 0) throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(opening));
        _balance = opening;
    }

    public decimal Balance => _balance;      // read-only to the outside

    public void Withdraw(decimal amount)
    {
        if (amount <= 0) throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(amount));
        if (amount > _balance) throw new InvalidOperationException("insufficient funds");
        _balance -= amount;                  // the invariant lives here, once
    }
}

// ABSTRACTION: callers know what an employee pays, not how it is worked out.
public abstract class Employee
{
    protected Employee(string name) => Name = name;
    public string Name { get; }

    public abstract decimal MonthlyPay();

    public override string ToString() => $"{Name}: {MonthlyPay():F2}";
}

// INHERITANCE: the name, the ToString and the contract are shared.
public class Salaried : Employee
{
    private readonly decimal _annual;
    public Salaried(string name, decimal annual) : base(name) => _annual = annual;
    public override decimal MonthlyPay() => _annual / 12;
}

public class Contractor : Employee
{
    private readonly decimal _rate;
    private readonly int _hours;
    public Contractor(string name, decimal rate, int hours) : base(name)
    { _rate = rate; _hours = hours; }
    public override decimal MonthlyPay() => _rate * _hours;
}

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var account = new Account(100m);
        account.Withdraw(30m);
        Console.WriteLine(account.Balance);

        try { account.Withdraw(1000m); }
        catch (InvalidOperationException e) { Console.WriteLine(e.Message); }

        // POLYMORPHISM: one loop, two different MonthlyPay implementations.
        var staff = new List<Employee>
        {
            new Salaried("Priya", 60000m),
            new Contractor("Sam", 50m, 160),
        };

        foreach (var e in staff) Console.WriteLine(e);

        decimal total = 0;
        foreach (var e in staff) total += e.MonthlyPay();
        Console.WriteLine($"total {total:F2}");
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
70
insufficient funds
Priya: 5000.00
Sam: 8000.00
total 13000.00
```

Store **`DateTimeOffset`** for anything representing an instant in time, and **`DateOnly`** for a calendar date with no time component. Reach for `DateTime` only when you have a specific reason.

**The mapping:**
- `DateTimeOffset` maps to `datetimeoffset` in SQL Server and `timestamptz` in PostgreSQL. The offset is preserved, so the value is unambiguous on its own.
- `DateTime` maps to `datetime2` (SQL Server) or `timestamp` (PostgreSQL). **The `Kind` is not stored.** A value saved as `Utc` comes back as `Unspecified`, and any later code calling `ToLocalTime()` on it silently produces the wrong answer.

That lost `Kind` is the whole problem. Nothing throws - you simply get times that are wrong by your server s offset, and only in some code paths.

```csharp
// If you must use DateTime, force UTC on the way in and out with a converter.
protected override void ConfigureConventions(ModelConfigurationBuilder builder)
{
    builder.Properties<DateTime>()
           .HaveConversion<UtcDateTimeConverter>();
}

public class UtcDateTimeConverter : ValueConverter<DateTime, DateTime>
{
    public UtcDateTimeConverter() : base(
        v => v.ToUniversalTime(),                                   // to database
        v => DateTime.SpecifyKind(v, DateTimeKind.Utc))             // from database
    { }
}

// Preferred: no converter needed, the offset round-trips
public class Order
{
    public DateTimeOffset CreatedUtc { get; set; }
    public DateOnly InvoiceDate { get; set; }          // a date, not an instant
}
```

**The distinction worth stating:** `DateTimeOffset` records an offset, **not a timezone**. It tells you the instant unambiguously, but not the zone s rules - so it cannot tell you what local time a future appointment will be after a DST change. For future scheduled events, store the local time plus the IANA timezone id and resolve at read time.

Design it as **keyset pagination with an opaque cursor**, and make the endpoint incapable of expressing the expensive cases.

**The shape of the endpoint:**
- `limit`, validated and capped server-side - a client asking for 10,000 gets 100.
- `cursor`, opaque and signed, carrying the last sort key plus the sort definition.
- No `page` parameter at all, so deep offsets are unrepresentable.

```csharp
public record PagedResult<T>(IReadOnlyList<T> Items, string NextCursor);

public async Task<PagedResult<OrderDto>> GetOrdersAsync(
    int customerId, string cursor, int limit, CancellationToken ct)
{
    limit = Math.Clamp(limit, 1, 100);                 // never trust the client

    var query = _db.Orders
        .AsNoTracking()                                 // read path: no change tracking
        .Where(o => o.CustomerId == customerId);

    if (cursor is not null)
    {
        var (lastCreated, lastId) = CursorCodec.Decode(cursor, "createdAt-desc");
        query = query.Where(o => o.CreatedUtc < lastCreated
                              || (o.CreatedUtc == lastCreated && o.Id < lastId));
    }

    // Order by a UNIQUE composite key, project to a DTO, and read one extra
    // row to discover whether another page exists - no COUNT(*) required.
    var rows = await query
        .OrderByDescending(o => o.CreatedUtc).ThenByDescending(o => o.Id)
        .Select(o => new OrderDto(o.Id, o.CreatedUtc, o.Total))
        .Take(limit + 1)
        .ToListAsync(ct);

    string next = null;
    if (rows.Count > limit)
    {
        rows.RemoveAt(limit);
        var last = rows[^1];
        next = CursorCodec.Encode("createdAt-desc", last.CreatedUtc, last.Id);
    }

    return new PagedResult<OrderDto>(rows, next);
}
```

**The four decisions that make this scale:**
1. **`Select` to a DTO**, so EF emits only the needed columns and lazy loading can never fire an N+1.
2. **A unique ordering** - `CreatedUtc` plus `Id` - so rows cannot shuffle between pages.
3. **`Take(limit + 1)`** instead of a `COUNT(*)`, which on a large filtered set can cost more than the page.
4. **A covering index on `(CustomerId, CreatedUtc DESC, Id DESC)`**, without which every page sorts the whole set and the design achieves nothing.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var fromFor = new List<Action>();
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) fromFor.Add(() => Console.Write(i + " "));

        var fromForeach = new List<Action>();
        foreach (int i in new[] { 0, 1, 2 }) fromForeach.Add(() => Console.Write(i + " "));

        foreach (var a in fromFor) a();
        Console.WriteLine();

        foreach (var a in fromForeach) a();
        Console.WriteLine();
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
3 3 3 
0 1 2 
```

**Why**

A `for` loop has one variable `i` for the whole loop, so all three lambdas close over the *same* variable. By the time they run, the loop has finished and `i` holds 3.

A `foreach` loop declares a fresh variable per iteration, so each lambda captures a different one. This was changed in C# 5: before that, `foreach` behaved like `for` and printed `2 2 2`, which was one of the most reported bugs in the language.

The fix for the `for` case is to copy into a local inside the body: `int copy = i;` and capture `copy`.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public struct Counter
{
    public int Value;
    public void Increment() => Value++;
}

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var array = new Counter[1];
        array[0].Increment();
        Console.WriteLine(array[0].Value);

        var list = new List<Counter> { new Counter() };
        list[0].Increment();          // operates on a COPY
        Console.WriteLine(list[0].Value);

        // The working pattern for a list: read, mutate, write back.
        var item = list[0];
        item.Increment();
        list[0] = item;
        Console.WriteLine(list[0].Value);

        Counter a = new Counter();
        Counter b = a;                 // value copy, not a reference
        b.Increment();
        Console.WriteLine($"{a.Value} {b.Value}");
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
1
0
1
0 1
```

**Why**

An array indexer returns a *reference* to the element, so `array[0].Increment()` mutates the stored struct. A `List<T>` indexer is a property returning a *copy*, so the increment happens on a temporary that is immediately discarded.

This is the main argument against mutable structs: the same syntax means different things depending on the container. `a` and `b` differ for the same reason — assignment copies the value.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    private static int ReturnFromTry()
    {
        int value = 1;
        try { return value; }         // the return VALUE is captured here
        finally { value = 2; }        // too late to change what was returned
    }

    private static int Order()
    {
        try
        {
            Console.Write("try ");
            throw new InvalidOperationException();
        }
        catch (InvalidOperationException)
        {
            Console.Write("catch ");
            return 1;
        }
        finally
        {
            Console.Write("finally ");   // runs BEFORE the method actually returns
        }
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(ReturnFromTry());

        int result = Order();
        Console.WriteLine(result);

        try
        {
            try { throw new ArgumentException("inner"); }
            finally { Console.Write("cleanup "); }
        }
        catch (ArgumentException e) { Console.WriteLine(e.Message); }
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
1
try catch finally 1
cleanup inner
```

**Why**

`return value` evaluates the expression and stores the result *before* `finally` runs, so reassigning the local afterwards changes nothing. The returned 1 was already captured.

In `Order`, the `finally` block runs after the `catch` completes but before control leaves the method, which is why `finally` prints before the caller sees the value.

A `finally` with no matching `catch` still runs as the exception propagates outward, which is what makes it reliable for cleanup.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Text;

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        string a = "hello";
        string b = "hello";
        Console.WriteLine(ReferenceEquals(a, b));      // both point at the intern pool

        string c = new StringBuilder().Append("hel").Append("lo").ToString();
        Console.WriteLine(ReferenceEquals(a, c));      // built at run time
        Console.WriteLine(a == c);                      // == compares VALUE for strings
        Console.WriteLine(a.Equals(c));

        string d = string.Intern(c);
        Console.WriteLine(ReferenceEquals(a, d));       // now it is the pooled instance

        // Compile-time constant folding: this IS interned.
        string e = "hel" + "lo";
        Console.WriteLine(ReferenceEquals(a, e));

        object boxedA = a;
        object boxedC = c;
        Console.WriteLine(boxedA == boxedC);            // object == is REFERENCE equality
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
True
False
True
True
True
True
False
```

**Why**

Identical string literals are interned by the runtime, so `a` and `b` are the same object. A string built at run time is a new instance, so `ReferenceEquals` is false even though the contents match.

`==` on `string` is overloaded to compare values, which is why `a == c` is true. But once both are typed as `object`, the overload no longer applies and `==` falls back to reference equality, giving false. That last line is the trap.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var numbers = new List<int> { 1, 2, 3 };

        var query = numbers.Where(n => n > 1);     // nothing executes yet
        var snapshot = numbers.Where(n => n > 1).ToList();   // executes now

        numbers.Add(4);

        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", query));      // sees the new item
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", snapshot));   // frozen at creation

        int threshold = 2;
        var captured = numbers.Where(n => n > threshold);
        threshold = 3;
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", captured));   // the lambda reads it at run time

        // Multiple enumeration re-runs the whole query.
        int calls = 0;
        var counted = numbers.Where(n => { calls++; return true; });
        counted.Count();
        counted.Count();
        Console.WriteLine(calls);
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
2,3,4
2,3
4
8
```

**Why**

`Where` builds a query object; it does not run until enumerated. So `query` observes the list as it is at enumeration time and includes the 4, while `ToList` materialised immediately and is frozen.

The captured `threshold` is read when the lambda executes, which is after it was changed to 3, so only 4 qualifies.

Enumerating twice runs the predicate twice over four items, hence eight calls. That is the multiple-enumeration trap, and it is why an expensive or side-effecting source should be materialised once.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class Solution
{
    private static async Task<int> Compute()
    {
        await Task.Yield();
        return 42;
    }

    private static async Task Throws()
    {
        await Task.Yield();
        throw new InvalidOperationException("from async Task");
    }

    public static async Task Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(await Compute());

        // An un-awaited task's exception stays dormant inside it.
        Task faulted = Throws();
        Console.WriteLine("not thrown yet");

        try { await faulted; }
        catch (InvalidOperationException e) { Console.WriteLine(e.Message); }

        Console.WriteLine(faulted.IsFaulted);

        // .Result wraps the exception in an AggregateException.
        var second = Throws();
        try { second.Wait(); }
        catch (AggregateException e)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"{e.GetType().Name} -> {e.InnerException.GetType().Name}");
        }
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
42
not thrown yet
from async Task
True
AggregateException -> InvalidOperationException
```

**Why**

An `async Task` method captures its exception inside the returned Task rather than throwing at the call site, so nothing surfaces until the task is awaited. Awaiting rethrows the original exception with its stack trace preserved.

`Wait()` and `.Result` instead wrap everything in an `AggregateException`, which is why the same failure looks different depending on how you consume the task — and why `await` is preferred.

An `async void` method, by contrast, has nowhere to put the exception: it is raised on the synchronization context and usually crashes the process. That is why `async void` is reserved for event handlers.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Base
{
    public Base()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Base ctor");
        Describe();                       // calls the OVERRIDE, on a half-built object
    }

    public virtual void Describe() => Console.WriteLine("Base.Describe");
}

public class Derived : Base
{
    private readonly string _fromInitialiser = "set by field initialiser";
    private readonly string _fromBody;                 // assigned in the ctor body

    public Derived()
    {
        _fromBody = "set by constructor body";
        Console.WriteLine("Derived ctor");
    }

    public override void Describe() =>
        Console.WriteLine($"Describe: initialiser={_fromInitialiser ?? "null"}, " +
                          $"body={_fromBody ?? "null"}");
}

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var d = new Derived();
        Console.WriteLine("---");
        d.Describe();
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
Base ctor
Describe: initialiser=set by field initialiser, body=null
Derived ctor
---
Describe: initialiser=set by field initialiser, body=set by constructor body
```

**Why**

Virtual dispatch uses the runtime type from the moment the object exists, so the base constructor calls the *derived* override before the derived constructor has run.

The two fields show the ordering precisely. In C# the derived **field initialisers run first**, before the base constructor, so `_fromInitialiser` already has its value. Anything assigned in the derived **constructor body** has not run yet, so `_fromBody` is null.

That partial initialisation is the hazard, and it is worse in Java, where field initialisers run *after* the super constructor and both fields would be null. The rule in either language: never call a virtual member from a constructor.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        int? a = null;
        int? b = 5;

        Console.WriteLine(a + b);                  // lifted: null propagates
        Console.WriteLine((a + b) ?? -1);
        Console.WriteLine(a ?? 0);

        Console.WriteLine(a > 0);                  // comparison with null is FALSE
        Console.WriteLine(a <= 0);                 // also false: not the negation
        Console.WriteLine(a == null);

        int? c = null;
        int? d = null;
        Console.WriteLine(c == d);                 // both null: equal

        Console.WriteLine(a.GetValueOrDefault());
        Console.WriteLine(b.Value + 1);

        // A nullable that holds a value boxes to the UNDERLYING type.
        object boxed = b;
        Console.WriteLine(boxed.GetType().Name);

        object boxedNull = a;
        Console.WriteLine(boxedNull == null);      // a null nullable boxes to null
    }
}
```

**Output**

```

-1
0
False
False
True
True
0
6
Int32
True
```

**Why**

Arithmetic operators are *lifted* over nullable types: if either operand is null the result is null, which prints as an empty line.

Comparison operators are lifted differently — any comparison involving null is false, so `a > 0` and `a <= 0` are **both** false. They are not negations of each other, which breaks the usual assumption and is the trap in this question.

Equality is the exception: two nulls compare equal. And boxing a nullable yields either null or a boxed value of the underlying type, never a boxed `Nullable<int>`.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var d = new Dictionary<string, int>();
        d["z"] = 1; d["a"] = 2; d["m"] = 3;

        // Looks like insertion order, but nothing guarantees it.
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", d.Keys));

        d.Remove("a");
        d["b"] = 4;              // reuses the freed slot: order now surprises
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", d.Keys));

        // Sorted alternatives, where order IS part of the contract.
        var sorted = new SortedDictionary<string, int>(d);
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", sorted.Keys));

        // The safe way to get deterministic output from a Dictionary.
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", d.Keys.OrderBy(k => k, StringComparer.Ordinal)));

        // Overwriting an existing key is allowed on .NET Core (it was not on
        // .NET Framework). Adding a key still invalidates the enumerator.
        foreach (var kv in d) d[kv.Key] = 0;
        Console.WriteLine("overwrite during enumeration: allowed");

        try
        {
            foreach (var kv in d) d["new"] = 1;
        }
        catch (InvalidOperationException)
        {
            Console.WriteLine("add during enumeration: throws");
        }
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
z,a,m
z,b,m
b,m,z
b,m,z
overwrite during enumeration: allowed
add during enumeration: throws
```

**Why**

`Dictionary<K,V>` appears to preserve insertion order while only additions occur, because entries are appended to an internal array. After a removal, the freed slot goes on a free list and the next insertion reuses it, so `b` lands where `a` was. The apparent ordering was never a guarantee.

The documented contract is explicit: the order is unspecified and must not be relied upon. If order matters, use `SortedDictionary`, or sort explicitly.

Overwriting an existing value during enumeration is permitted on .NET Core, though it threw on .NET Framework. Adding a key is a structural change and still invalidates the enumerator. That the same code behaves differently by runtime is itself the warning.

**What's happening**

A captive dependency. The Singleton is created once and holds the *first* DbContext it was given forever, so the Scoped lifetime is effectively promoted to Singleton. Every request then shares one DbContext.

That causes both symptoms. DbContext has a first-level cache of tracked entities, so one user's loaded entities are visible to the next — that is the data leak. And DbContext is not thread-safe, so two concurrent requests hitting it produce the 'second operation' exception.

It works locally because you are the only user: one request at a time, and the stale cache never shows.

**How to confirm**

Call `services.BuildServiceProvider(new ServiceProviderOptions { ValidateScopes = true })`, or run in Development where ASP.NET Core enables scope validation by default — it throws at startup with the exact offending pair. That check is *disabled in Production*, which is why the bug reached it silently.

**The fix**

Either make the consumer Scoped, or if it genuinely must be a Singleton, inject `IServiceScopeFactory` and create a scope per unit of work. Never inject a Scoped service into a Singleton constructor.

**Demonstration**

```csharp
using System;
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;

public class ScopedThing
{
    private static int _created;
    public int Id { get; } = ++_created;
}

// The bug: captures whatever instance it was constructed with, forever.
public class CaptiveConsumer
{
    private readonly ScopedThing _captured;
    public CaptiveConsumer(ScopedThing thing) { _captured = thing; }
    public int SeenId => _captured.Id;
}

// The fix: resolve per unit of work from a scope factory.
public class CorrectConsumer
{
    private readonly IServiceScopeFactory _scopes;
    public CorrectConsumer(IServiceScopeFactory scopes) { _scopes = scopes; }

    public int SeenId
    {
        get
        {
            using var scope = _scopes.CreateScope();
            return scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<ScopedThing>().Id;
        }
    }
}

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var services = new ServiceCollection();
        services.AddScoped<ScopedThing>();
        services.AddSingleton<CaptiveConsumer>();
        services.AddSingleton<CorrectConsumer>();

        var provider = services.BuildServiceProvider();

        // Two "requests".
        for (int request = 1; request <= 2; request++)
        {
            using var scope = provider.CreateScope();

            int fresh = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<ScopedThing>().Id;
            int captive = provider.GetRequiredService<CaptiveConsumer>().SeenId;
            int correct = provider.GetRequiredService<CorrectConsumer>().SeenId;

            Console.WriteLine($"request {request}: scoped instance {fresh}, " +
                              $"captive sees {captive}, corrected sees {correct}");
        }

        Console.WriteLine();

        // Scope validation catches it at startup - but only when enabled.
        try
        {
            var validated = services.BuildServiceProvider(
                new ServiceProviderOptions { ValidateScopes = true, ValidateOnBuild = true });

            validated.GetRequiredService<CaptiveConsumer>();
            Console.WriteLine("validation did not complain");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"ValidateOnBuild caught it: {ex.InnerExceptions[0].GetType().Name}");
        }
        catch (InvalidOperationException ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"validation caught it: {ex.GetType().Name}");
        }
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
request 1: scoped instance 1, captive sees 2, corrected sees 3
request 2: scoped instance 4, captive sees 2, corrected sees 5

ValidateOnBuild caught it: InvalidOperationException
```

The captive consumer reports the same instance on both requests while everything else advances — that is the leak, made visible.

**What's happening**

`async void` has no Task to carry the exception. When an `async Task` method throws, the exception is captured and placed on the returned Task, where the caller observes it. With `async void` there is no such object, so the exception is re-thrown on the captured SynchronizationContext — and in a console or worker process there isn't one, so it goes to the thread pool and takes the process down.

That is why nothing useful reaches the log: the crash happens on a thread nobody is awaiting, outside any try/catch you wrote around the call site.

**How to confirm**

Wrapping the call in try/catch does nothing — that is the diagnostic. Subscribe to `AppDomain.CurrentDomain.UnhandledException` and you will see it arrive there instead of at your call site.

**The fix**

Make it `async Task` and await it. Where a void signature is forced on you by an event handler, catch everything *inside* the handler — the exception must never be allowed to escape an `async void` method.

**Demonstration**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

// Stands in for a UI or host context. Without one, an async void throw kills the process.
public class CapturingContext : SynchronizationContext
{
    public string Seen { get; private set; }

    public override void Post(SendOrPostCallback d, object state)
    {
        try { d(state); }
        catch (Exception ex) { Seen = ex.Message; }
    }
}

public class Solution
{
    private static async Task ThrowsAsync()
    {
        await Task.Yield();
        throw new InvalidOperationException("the real failure");
    }

    // The bug: nothing to catch, because there is no Task to observe.
    private static async void FireAndForget() => await ThrowsAsync();

    // The fix where a void signature is unavoidable: contain it at the boundary.
    private static async void ContainedHandler()
    {
        try { await ThrowsAsync(); }
        catch (Exception ex) { Console.WriteLine($"handler logged: {ex.Message}"); }
    }

    public static async Task Main()
    {
        // An async Task exception is observable at the call site.
        try { await ThrowsAsync(); }
        catch (InvalidOperationException ex) { Console.WriteLine($"async Task: caught [{ex.Message}]"); }

        // The same call as async void escapes the try entirely. A SynchronizationContext
        // catches it here so the demo can report it - without one the process simply dies.
        var context = new CapturingContext();
        SynchronizationContext.SetSynchronizationContext(context);

        bool caughtAtCallSite = false;

        try { FireAndForget(); }
        catch (Exception) { caughtAtCallSite = true; }

        await Task.Delay(200);
        SynchronizationContext.SetSynchronizationContext(null);

        Console.WriteLine($"async void: caught at the call site? {caughtAtCallSite}");
        Console.WriteLine($"async void: surfaced on the context instead? {context.Seen != null}");
        Console.WriteLine($"async void: the message was [{context.Seen}]");

        ContainedHandler();
        await Task.Delay(200);

        Console.WriteLine("the contained handler never escapes");
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
async Task: caught [the real failure]
async void: caught at the call site? False
async void: surfaced on the context instead? True
async void: the message was [the real failure]
handler logged: the real failure
the contained handler never escapes
```

The try/catch around the `async void` call catches nothing — the exception surfaces on a completely different path.

**What's happening**

The object was placed in a bucket determined by its hash code at insertion time. Mutating a property that `GetHashCode` depends on changes the hash, so the lookup now computes a *different* bucket and finds nothing there. The object is still in the set — enumeration walks every bucket, which is why you can see it — but it is unreachable by hash.

This is the mutable-key problem, and it applies equally to Dictionary keys.

**How to confirm**

Print `GetHashCode()` before and after the mutation. If it changed, the entry is orphaned.

**The fix**

Make anything used as a hash key immutable — `init`-only properties or a readonly record struct. If mutation is genuinely required, remove the object from the set first, mutate it, and re-add it. Alternatively base the hash on an identity that never changes, such as a database id, and not on the mutable business fields.

**Demonstration**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class MutableKey
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public MutableKey(string name) { Name = name; }

    public override int GetHashCode() => Name?.GetHashCode() ?? 0;
    public override bool Equals(object obj) => obj is MutableKey other && other.Name == Name;
}

// Identity that cannot drift: the hash is fixed at construction.
public class StableKey
{
    public int Id { get; }
    public string Name { get; set; }   // free to change; not part of the identity
    public StableKey(int id, string name) { Id = id; Name = name; }

    public override int GetHashCode() => Id;
    public override bool Equals(object obj) => obj is StableKey other && other.Id == Id;
}

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var broken = new MutableKey("before");
        var set = new HashSet<MutableKey> { broken };

        Console.WriteLine($"contains before mutation: {set.Contains(broken)}");

        int hashBefore = broken.GetHashCode();
        broken.Name = "after";
        int hashAfter = broken.GetHashCode();

        Console.WriteLine($"hash changed: {hashBefore != hashAfter}");
        Console.WriteLine($"contains after mutation: {set.Contains(broken)}");
        Console.WriteLine($"but enumeration still finds it: {set.Any(k => ReferenceEquals(k, broken))}");
        Console.WriteLine($"and Count still says: {set.Count}");

        // Removing it also fails - it looks in the wrong bucket too.
        Console.WriteLine($"Remove succeeds: {set.Remove(broken)}");
        Console.WriteLine($"Count after the failed remove: {set.Count}");

        Console.WriteLine();

        var stable = new StableKey(7, "before");
        var good = new HashSet<StableKey> { stable };

        stable.Name = "after";

        Console.WriteLine($"stable identity - contains after mutation: {good.Contains(stable)}");
        Console.WriteLine($"stable identity - remove succeeds: {good.Remove(stable)}");
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
contains before mutation: True
hash changed: True
contains after mutation: False
but enumeration still finds it: True
and Count still says: 1
Remove succeeds: False
Count after the failed remove: 1

stable identity - contains after mutation: True
stable identity - remove succeeds: True
```

Note that `Remove` fails too — so the entry can never be taken out by reference, which in a long-lived set is a memory leak.

**What's happening**

The catch block rethrows with `throw ex;` instead of `throw;`. That resets the stack trace to the rethrow point, discarding everything below it — which is exactly the part that tells you where the failure came from.

It is a one-character difference with a large operational cost: the log now says the error happened in the retry wrapper, which is never true and never useful.

**How to confirm**

Compare the first line of the stack trace with the method that logged it. If they are the same and the method is a wrapper, the trace has been truncated.

**The fix**

Use bare `throw;` to preserve the original trace. If the exception must cross a thread or be stored and rethrown later, use `ExceptionDispatchInfo.Capture(ex).Throw()`, which preserves the original trace *and* appends the rethrow location.

Wrapping in a new exception is also fine — provided the original is passed as the inner exception, so nothing is lost.

**Demonstration**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Runtime.ExceptionServices;

public class Solution
{
    private static void DeepFailure() => throw new InvalidOperationException("original failure");

    private static void Middle() => DeepFailure();

    private static int FrameCount(Exception ex) =>
        (ex.StackTrace ?? "").Split('\n', StringSplitOptions.RemoveEmptyEntries).Length;

    private static bool MentionsOrigin(Exception ex) =>
        (ex.StackTrace ?? "").Contains("DeepFailure");

    public static void Main()
    {
        // The bug: throw ex; resets the trace.
        try
        {
            try { Middle(); }
            catch (Exception ex) { throw ex; }
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"throw ex;   frames {FrameCount(ex)}, " +
                              $"mentions the origin: {MentionsOrigin(ex)}");
        }

        // The fix: bare throw preserves it.
        try
        {
            try { Middle(); }
            catch (Exception) { throw; }
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"throw;      frames {FrameCount(ex)}, " +
                              $"mentions the origin: {MentionsOrigin(ex)}");
        }

        // Across a boundary: capture and rethrow keeps the trace too.
        ExceptionDispatchInfo captured = null;

        try { Middle(); }
        catch (Exception ex) { captured = ExceptionDispatchInfo.Capture(ex); }

        try { captured.Throw(); }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Capture().Throw() mentions the origin: {MentionsOrigin(ex)}");
        }

        // Wrapping is fine as long as the original is the inner exception.
        try
        {
            try { Middle(); }
            catch (Exception ex) { throw new ApplicationException("while retrying", ex); }
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"wrapped: inner preserved: {MentionsOrigin(ex.InnerException)}, " +
                              $"inner message [{ex.InnerException.Message}]");
        }
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
throw ex;   frames 1, mentions the origin: False
throw;      frames 3, mentions the origin: True
Capture().Throw() mentions the origin: True
wrapped: inner preserved: True, inner message [original failure]
```

One frame versus three, and the method that actually failed has vanished from the trace entirely.

**What's happening**

Deferred execution. An `IEnumerable<T>` is not data — it is a recipe. Every time you enumerate it, the recipe runs again. `Count()` enumerates once, the `foreach` enumerates again, so a database-backed sequence is queried twice.

The second symptom follows from the same cause. If the underlying source can change between the two enumerations — a query against live data, a generator reading a file, a sequence over a mutable collection — the two passes legitimately see different results. The count was never a property of the data; it was the answer to a question asked at a particular moment.

**How to confirm**

Wrap the source in a counter that increments every time `GetEnumerator` is called. If it is called more than once per operation, you are re-enumerating.

**The fix**

Materialise once with `ToList()` and work with that, or take `IReadOnlyCollection<T>`/`IReadOnlyList<T>` in the signature so the caller is responsible for materialising and `Count` is a property rather than a traversal.

Also prefer `Any()` to `Count() > 0` — `Any()` stops after the first element, while `Count()` walks the entire sequence just to compare against zero.

**Demonstration**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

// Counts how many times something asks it to enumerate.
public class CountingSource : IEnumerable<int>
{
    private readonly List<int> _items;
    public int EnumerationCount { get; private set; }

    public CountingSource(params int[] items) { _items = items.ToList(); }

    public IEnumerator<int> GetEnumerator()
    {
        EnumerationCount++;
        return _items.GetEnumerator();
    }

    IEnumerator IEnumerable.GetEnumerator() => GetEnumerator();

    public void SimulateExternalChange() => _items.Clear();
}

public class Solution
{
    // A real iterator, so nothing can shortcut it with a known count.
    private static IEnumerable<int> Noisy()
    {
        for (int v = 1; v <= 5; v++) { Console.Write($"[{v}]"); yield return v; }
    }

    private static void Process(IEnumerable<int> items, CountingSource probe)
    {
        if (items.Count() > 0)
            foreach (int _ in items) { }

        Console.WriteLine($"  enumerated {probe.EnumerationCount} time(s)");
    }

    private static void ProcessFixed(IEnumerable<int> items, CountingSource probe)
    {
        var materialised = items.ToList();   // one pass, then work with the data

        if (materialised.Count > 0)
            foreach (int _ in materialised) { }

        Console.WriteLine($"  enumerated {probe.EnumerationCount} time(s)");
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Count() then foreach:");
        var a = new CountingSource(1, 2, 3);
        Process(a, a);

        Console.WriteLine("ToList() first:");
        var b = new CountingSource(1, 2, 3);
        ProcessFixed(b, b);

        Console.WriteLine();

        // Any() stops at the first element; Count() walks everything.
        var lazy = Noisy();
        Console.Write("Any():   "); _ = lazy.Any(); Console.WriteLine();
        Console.Write("Count(): "); _ = lazy.Count(); Console.WriteLine();

        Console.WriteLine();

        // The source changed between the two passes.
        var shifting = new CountingSource(1, 2, 3);
        int firstAnswer = shifting.Count();
        shifting.SimulateExternalChange();
        int secondAnswer = shifting.Count();

        Console.WriteLine($"same sequence, two enumerations: {firstAnswer} then {secondAnswer}");
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
Count() then foreach:
  enumerated 2 time(s)
ToList() first:
  enumerated 1 time(s)

Any():   [1]
Count(): [1][2][3][4][5]

same sequence, two enumerations: 3 then 0
```

`Any()` touched one element where `Count()` touched five, and the same sequence honestly reported 3 and then 0.

**What's happening**

Almost certainly an N+1: the outer loop runs one query per row rather than one query for everything. Two hundred rows becomes two hundred and one round trips, and at ~10ms of network latency each that is the 3 seconds — with the database itself barely working, which is exactly the symptom described. Database CPU being fine is the tell that this is round-trip count, not query cost.

**How to confirm**

Turn on EF Core query logging and count the statements for one request, or watch the query count in Application Insights / MiniProfiler. If the number of queries scales with the number of rows, it is N+1. A single request issuing hundreds of identical queries with only the parameter changing is conclusive.

**The fix**

Fetch the related data in one query. Either `Include` the navigation so EF joins it, or — usually better for a large set — load the lookup keys into a dictionary up front and resolve from memory inside the loop.

**Demonstration**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

// Stands in for the database: counts round trips.
public class FakeRepository
{
    private readonly Dictionary<int, string> _names = new();
    public int Queries { get; private set; }

    public FakeRepository(int rows)
    {
        for (int i = 1; i <= rows; i++) _names[i] = $"customer-{i}";
    }

    public string GetNameById(int id)
    {
        Queries++;
        return _names[id];
    }

    public Dictionary<int, string> GetNamesByIds(IEnumerable<int> ids)
    {
        Queries++;
        return ids.Distinct().ToDictionary(id => id, id => _names[id]);
    }
}

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        foreach (int rows in new[] { 10, 200 })
        {
            var orderIds = Enumerable.Range(1, rows).ToList();

            // The N+1: one lookup per row.
            var nPlusOne = new FakeRepository(rows);
            foreach (int id in orderIds) _ = nPlusOne.GetNameById(id);

            // Batched: one lookup, then resolve in memory.
            var batched = new FakeRepository(rows);
            var lookup = batched.GetNamesByIds(orderIds);
            foreach (int id in orderIds) _ = lookup[id];

            Console.WriteLine($"{rows,4} rows - per-row queries: {nPlusOne.Queries,4}, " +
                              $"batched: {batched.Queries}");

            // At 10ms of round-trip latency each.
            Console.WriteLine($"       at 10ms per round trip: " +
                              $"{nPlusOne.Queries * 10}ms versus {batched.Queries * 10}ms");
        }
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
  10 rows - per-row queries:   10, batched: 1
       at 10ms per round trip: 100ms versus 10ms
 200 rows - per-row queries:  200, batched: 1
       at 10ms per round trip: 2000ms versus 10ms
```

The query count scales with the row count, which is the signature of the problem — and it explains why it passed review and QA, where the test data had ten rows.

**What's happening**

No — this is documented behaviour. `GetOrAdd` does not hold a lock while running the value factory. Several threads can observe the key as absent, all invoke the factory, and then exactly one of their results is stored while the others are discarded.

So the dictionary's *state* is correct — every caller gets the same value back — but the factory ran several times. When the factory is pure that is merely wasteful. When it has side effects, opens a connection, or costs money per call, it is a real bug.

**How to confirm**

Increment a counter inside the factory and compare it with the number of distinct keys. Under concurrency the counter exceeds the key count.

**The fix**

Store a `Lazy<T>` rather than the value. Constructing the `Lazy` is cheap so the redundant work becomes negligible, and with `ExecutionAndPublication` — the default — only one thread ever runs the real factory. For async factories, store `Task<T>` or `AsyncLazy<T>`.

**Demonstration**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Concurrent;
using System.Linq;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        const int threads = 16;

        // The problem: the factory is not serialised.
        var plain = new ConcurrentDictionary<string, string>();
        int plainCalls = 0;

        var ready = new ManualResetEventSlim();

        Parallel.For(0, threads, worker =>
        {
            ready.Set();
            plain.GetOrAdd("key", key =>
            {
                Interlocked.Increment(ref plainCalls);
                Thread.Sleep(50);              // an expensive build
                return "value";
            });
        });

        // The fix: the factory lives inside a Lazy, which serialises it.
        var lazyCache = new ConcurrentDictionary<string, Lazy<string>>();
        int lazyCalls = 0;

        Parallel.For(0, threads, worker =>
        {
            var holder = lazyCache.GetOrAdd("key", key => new Lazy<string>(() =>
            {
                Interlocked.Increment(ref lazyCalls);
                Thread.Sleep(50);
                return "value";
            }, LazyThreadSafetyMode.ExecutionAndPublication));

            _ = holder.Value;   // only one thread runs the body
        });

        // The exact count varies between runs - only the PROPERTY is stable.
        Console.WriteLine($"{threads} threads racing on one key");
        Console.WriteLine($"  plain factory ran more than once: {plainCalls > 1}");
        Console.WriteLine($"  Lazy factory ran exactly once:    {lazyCalls == 1}");

        // Either way the stored value is consistent for every caller.
        Console.WriteLine($"  every caller saw the same value: " +
                          $"{plain["key"] == "value" && lazyCache["key"].Value == "value"}");
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
16 threads racing on one key
  plain factory ran more than once: True
  Lazy factory ran exactly once:    True
  every caller saw the same value: True
```

The plain factory runs repeatedly - how many times varies between runs, which is why the demonstration asserts the property rather than a count - while the `Lazy` version runs exactly once every time.

**What's happening**

Culture-sensitive casing. In Turkish, the uppercase of 'i' is 'İ' (dotted capital I), not 'I'. So `"admin".ToUpper()` under a Turkish culture produces `"ADMİN"`, which does not equal `"ADMIN"` — and the check fails for legitimate users. This is the classic Turkish-I problem.

The security angle is the same mechanism in reverse: casing behaviour that varies by the ambient culture means an authorisation decision depends on the server's locale, or on a thread's culture that a request can influence. Comparisons that gate access must not depend on ambient state.

**How to confirm**

Set `CultureInfo.CurrentCulture` to `tr-TR` and re-run the check. If the result changes, the comparison is culture-sensitive.

**The fix**

Use `string.Equals(input, "ADMIN", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)`. Ordinal comparison is byte-wise, culture-independent, faster, and allocates nothing — casing the string to compare it allocates a new string every call.

The rule: linguistic comparison for anything a human reads and sorts; ordinal for identifiers, tokens, protocol values and anything security-relevant.

**Demonstration**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Globalization;
using System.Threading;

public class Solution
{
    private static bool ByCasing(string input) => input.ToUpper() == "ADMIN";

    private static bool ByOrdinal(string input) =>
        string.Equals(input, "ADMIN", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase);

    private static void Check(string cultureName)
    {
        Thread.CurrentThread.CurrentCulture = new CultureInfo(cultureName);

        const string input = "admin";

        Console.WriteLine($"{cultureName,-6} ToUpper() gives [{input.ToUpper()}]  " +
                          $"casing check: {ByCasing(input),-5} ordinal check: {ByOrdinal(input)}");
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Check("en-US");
        Check("tr-TR");

        Console.WriteLine();

        // The invariant culture is safe for casing but still the wrong tool for equality.
        Thread.CurrentThread.CurrentCulture = new CultureInfo("tr-TR");
        Console.WriteLine($"ToUpperInvariant() under tr-TR: [{"admin".ToUpperInvariant()}]");

        // Sorting is where culture-sensitive comparison is the CORRECT choice.
        var words = new[] { "resume", "résumé", "Resume" };
        Array.Sort(words, StringComparer.Ordinal);
        Console.WriteLine($"ordinal sort:  {string.Join(",", words)}");

        Array.Sort(words, StringComparer.Create(new CultureInfo("en-US"), true));
        Console.WriteLine($"cultural sort: {string.Join(",", words)}");
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
en-US  ToUpper() gives [ADMIN]  casing check: True  ordinal check: True
tr-TR  ToUpper() gives [ADMİN]  casing check: False ordinal check: True

ToUpperInvariant() under tr-TR: [ADMIN]
ordinal sort:  Resume,resume,résumé
cultural sort: Resume,resume,résumé
```

The casing check silently changes answer with the culture; the ordinal check does not.

**What's happening**

Connections are being taken from the pool and not returned. The pool defaults to 100 connections; once all are checked out, the next request waits and then times out. The message is about the *pool*, not the database, so the database being healthy is expected.

The usual cause is a code path that creates a connection or a DbContext without disposing it — an exception thrown between opening and closing, a missing `using`, or an async method whose disposal was skipped because the Task was never awaited.

**How to confirm**

Watch the `NumberOfActiveConnections` and `NumberOfFreeConnections` performance counters, or query `sys.dm_exec_sessions` for sessions from your application that are sleeping with no open transaction and growing in number. If the count only ever rises, something is leaking.

**The fix**

Every connection and DbContext in a `using`, so disposal happens on the exception path too. Where a scope owns it, let DI dispose it — which means the consumer must be Scoped, not Singleton.

**Demonstration**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

// Stands in for a pooled connection.
public class PooledConnection : IDisposable
{
    private readonly ConnectionPool _pool;
    public PooledConnection(ConnectionPool pool) { _pool = pool; }
    public void Dispose() => _pool.Return(this);
}

public class ConnectionPool
{
    private readonly int _size;
    private int _inUse;

    public ConnectionPool(int size) { _size = size; }
    public int InUse => _inUse;

    public PooledConnection Rent()
    {
        if (_inUse >= _size)
            throw new TimeoutException(
                "The timeout period elapsed prior to obtaining a connection from the pool.");

        _inUse++;
        return new PooledConnection(this);
    }

    public void Return(PooledConnection connection) => _inUse--;
}

public class Solution
{
    // The leak: an exception escapes before Dispose is reached.
    private static void Leaky(ConnectionPool pool, bool fails)
    {
        var connection = pool.Rent();

        if (fails) throw new InvalidOperationException("query failed");

        connection.Dispose();
    }

    // The fix: using disposes on every path, including the exception path.
    private static void Correct(ConnectionPool pool, bool fails)
    {
        using var connection = pool.Rent();

        if (fails) throw new InvalidOperationException("query failed");
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        var leakyPool = new ConnectionPool(5);

        for (int request = 1; request <= 5; request++)
        {
            try { Leaky(leakyPool, fails: true); } catch (InvalidOperationException) { }
            Console.WriteLine($"leaky  - after request {request}: {leakyPool.InUse} in use");
        }

        try { Leaky(leakyPool, fails: false); }
        catch (TimeoutException ex) { Console.WriteLine($"  -> {ex.Message}"); }

        Console.WriteLine();

        var correctPool = new ConnectionPool(5);

        for (int request = 1; request <= 8; request++)
        {
            try { Correct(correctPool, fails: true); } catch (InvalidOperationException) { }
        }

        Console.WriteLine($"with using - after 8 failing requests: {correctPool.InUse} in use");
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
leaky  - after request 1: 1 in use
leaky  - after request 2: 2 in use
leaky  - after request 3: 3 in use
leaky  - after request 4: 4 in use
leaky  - after request 5: 5 in use
  -> The timeout period elapsed prior to obtaining a connection from the pool.

with using - after 8 failing requests: 0 in use
```

The leak only shows on the failure path — which is why it appeared under load, when failures became common enough to exhaust the pool.

**What's happening**

Structs are copied on assignment and on access. The `foreach` variable is a copy, which is why the compiler refuses to let you mutate it — it is protecting you from a change that would silently go nowhere.

`list[i]` is a method call, `get_Item`, which *returns* a copy. Mutating a property on that copy fails to compile for the same reason; mutating a public field compiles because the copy is an addressable value, but the change is written to the temporary and discarded.

An array behaves differently: `array[i]` is a direct element reference, not a call, so `array[i].Count++` genuinely mutates the element. That inconsistency between arrays and List is what makes this so confusing.

**How to confirm**

Do the same thing to an array and to a List and compare. The array mutates, the List does not.

**The fix**

Make the struct immutable and replace the whole element: `list[i] = list[i] with { Count = ... }`, or use `CollectionsMarshal.AsSpan(list)` to get a span over the backing store, whose indexer returns a `ref`. For most code, the honest answer is that a mutable struct in a collection is the wrong design — use a class or make the struct readonly.

**Demonstration**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;

public struct Counter
{
    public int Count;   // a public field, so mutation at least compiles
    public Counter(int count) { Count = count; }
}

public readonly record struct ImmutableCounter(int Count);

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        // An ARRAY element is a direct reference: mutation works.
        var array = new[] { new Counter(0), new Counter(0) };
        array[0].Count++;
        Console.WriteLine($"array element after ++ : {array[0].Count}");

        // A LIST indexer returns a copy: the mutation is thrown away.
        var list = new List<Counter> { new Counter(0), new Counter(0) };
        var copy = list[0];
        copy.Count++;
        Console.WriteLine($"list element after mutating a copy: {list[0].Count}");

        // Read, modify, write back - correct but easy to forget.
        var element = list[0];
        element.Count++;
        list[0] = element;
        Console.WriteLine($"list element after write-back: {list[0].Count}");

        // A span over the backing store gives a ref, so this mutates in place.
        var span = CollectionsMarshal.AsSpan(list);
        span[0].Count++;
        Console.WriteLine($"list element after span mutation: {list[0].Count}");

        Console.WriteLine();

        // The design that avoids the whole question.
        var immutable = new List<ImmutableCounter> { new(0) };
        immutable[0] = immutable[0] with { Count = immutable[0].Count + 1 };
        Console.WriteLine($"immutable struct, replaced: {immutable[0].Count}");

        // A struct in a foreach is always a copy, whatever the source.
        int untouched = 0;
        foreach (var item in array) untouched += item.Count;
        Console.WriteLine($"foreach reads copies, sum is still: {untouched}");
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
array element after ++ : 1
list element after mutating a copy: 0
list element after write-back: 1
list element after span mutation: 2

immutable struct, replaced: 1
foreach reads copies, sum is still: 1
```

The same expression shape mutates an array and silently does nothing to a List.

**What's happening**

The three interfaces differ in *when* the value is bound and how long it lives.

`IOptions<T>` is a singleton — bound once at first use and cached for the life of the application. It never sees a reload, which is exactly the reported symptom.

`IOptionsSnapshot<T>` is scoped — recomputed once per request, so a change is picked up on the next request. Being scoped, it cannot be injected into a singleton, which is the captive-dependency rule again and why the service broke.

`IOptionsMonitor<T>` is a singleton that reads the *current* value on every access and can notify on change. It is the one to use in singletons and background services.

**How to confirm**

Change the underlying configuration source and read each of the three. Only the monitor and a fresh snapshot reflect it.

**The fix**

Use `IOptionsMonitor<T>` in singletons and hosted services, `IOptionsSnapshot<T>` in per-request components where a value must be stable for the duration of the request, and `IOptions<T>` only for settings that genuinely never change.

**Demonstration**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration;
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Options;

public class FeatureSettings
{
    public bool Enabled { get; set; }
}

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var source = new Dictionary<string, string> { ["Feature:Enabled"] = "false" };

        var configuration = new ConfigurationBuilder()
            .AddInMemoryCollection(source)
            .Build();

        var services = new ServiceCollection();
        services.AddSingleton<IConfiguration>(configuration);
        services.Configure<FeatureSettings>(configuration.GetSection("Feature"));

        var provider = services.BuildServiceProvider();

        var options = provider.GetRequiredService<IOptions<FeatureSettings>>();
        var monitor = provider.GetRequiredService<IOptionsMonitor<FeatureSettings>>();

        // Force IOptions to bind and cache now.
        Console.WriteLine($"before change - IOptions {options.Value.Enabled}, " +
                          $"IOptionsMonitor {monitor.CurrentValue.Enabled}");

        int changeNotifications = 0;
        monitor.OnChange(_ => changeNotifications++);

        // Write through the configuration root so the provider actually sees it,
        // then reload - mutating the seed dictionary would change nothing.
        configuration["Feature:Enabled"] = "true";
        configuration.Reload();

        Console.WriteLine($"after change  - IOptions {options.Value.Enabled}  (cached forever), " +
                          $"IOptionsMonitor {monitor.CurrentValue.Enabled}");
        Console.WriteLine($"monitor raised a change notification: {changeNotifications > 0}");

        // A snapshot is per scope: a new scope sees the new value.
        using (var scope = provider.CreateScope())
        {
            var snapshot = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<IOptionsSnapshot<FeatureSettings>>();
            Console.WriteLine($"IOptionsSnapshot in a new scope: {snapshot.Value.Enabled}");
        }

        // And cannot be resolved from the root provider at all.
        try
        {
            _ = provider.GetRequiredService<IOptionsSnapshot<FeatureSettings>>().Value;
            Console.WriteLine("snapshot resolved from the root - no complaint");
        }
        catch (InvalidOperationException)
        {
            Console.WriteLine("snapshot from the root provider: rejected as expected");
        }
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
before change - IOptions False, IOptionsMonitor False
after change  - IOptions False  (cached forever), IOptionsMonitor True
monitor raised a change notification: True
IOptionsSnapshot in a new scope: True
snapshot resolved from the root - no complaint
```

`IOptions` is still reporting the old value after the reload; the monitor is not.

**What's happening**

Daylight saving. In a spring-forward transition, local time jumps from 02:00 to 03:00 — 02:30 simply does not exist that night, so a job scheduled for it never ran. In autumn, the clock goes back and 02:30 occurs twice, so it ran twice.

The negative durations come from the same cause: subtracting two `DateTime.Now` values across the autumn transition gives a negative interval, because local time went backwards.

`DateTime.Now` returns a value with `Kind = Local` and no offset, so once stored it is genuinely ambiguous — there is no way to tell which of the two 02:30s it was.

**How to confirm**

Ask the timezone directly: `TimeZoneInfo.IsInvalidTime` for a timestamp that cannot exist, `IsAmbiguousTime` for one that occurs twice.

**The fix**

Store and compute in UTC — `DateTime.UtcNow`, or better `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow`, which carries the offset so the instant is unambiguous. Convert to local time only at the point of display. For elapsed time, use `Stopwatch`, which is monotonic and unaffected by clock changes entirely.

For scheduling, express the rule in UTC or use a scheduler that understands timezone rules, and never schedule in the 02:00–03:00 local window.

**Demonstration**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        // A zone with a well-known DST rule.
        TimeZoneInfo zone;
        try { zone = TimeZoneInfo.FindSystemTimeZoneById("Europe/London"); }
        catch (TimeZoneNotFoundException) { zone = TimeZoneInfo.FindSystemTimeZoneById("GMT Standard Time"); }

        // Spring 2026: clocks go forward on 29 March at 01:00 UTC.
        var springGap = new DateTime(2026, 3, 29, 1, 30, 0, DateTimeKind.Unspecified);

        Console.WriteLine($"{springGap:yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm} local");
        Console.WriteLine($"  exists at all?  {!zone.IsInvalidTime(springGap)}");

        // Autumn 2026: clocks go back on 25 October at 02:00 local.
        var autumnRepeat = new DateTime(2026, 10, 25, 1, 30, 0, DateTimeKind.Unspecified);

        Console.WriteLine($"{autumnRepeat:yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm} local");
        Console.WriteLine($"  happens twice?  {zone.IsAmbiguousTime(autumnRepeat)}");

        var offsets = zone.GetAmbiguousTimeOffsets(autumnRepeat);
        Console.WriteLine($"  possible offsets: {string.Join(" and ", offsets)}");

        Console.WriteLine();

        // The negative duration: local time goes backwards across the transition.
        var startLocal = new DateTimeOffset(2026, 10, 25, 1, 30, 0, TimeSpan.FromHours(1));
        var endLocal = new DateTimeOffset(2026, 10, 25, 1, 10, 0, TimeSpan.FromHours(0));

        // Naive local subtraction, ignoring the offsets.
        TimeSpan naive = endLocal.DateTime - startLocal.DateTime;
        TimeSpan correct = endLocal - startLocal;

        Console.WriteLine($"local clock difference:   {naive.TotalMinutes} minutes");
        Console.WriteLine($"actual elapsed:           {correct.TotalMinutes} minutes");
        Console.WriteLine($"naive result is negative: {naive < TimeSpan.Zero}");

        Console.WriteLine();

        // UTC has no transitions, so arithmetic is always well defined.
        var utcStart = new DateTime(2026, 10, 25, 0, 30, 0, DateTimeKind.Utc);
        var utcEnd = utcStart.AddMinutes(40);
        Console.WriteLine($"the same span computed in UTC: {(utcEnd - utcStart).TotalMinutes} minutes");
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
2026-03-29 01:30 local
  exists at all?  False
2026-10-25 01:30 local
  happens twice?  True
  possible offsets: 00:00:00 and 01:00:00

local clock difference:   -20 minutes
actual elapsed:           40 minutes
naive result is negative: True

the same span computed in UTC: 40 minutes
```

The same forty minutes reads as minus twenty on the local clock.

**Why each one blocks testing**

**`DateTime.Now`** is a hidden dependency on the system clock. Any test involving a date boundary, an expiry or 'older than 30 days' is either non-deterministic or untestable, and will fail on a particular day of the month.

**Static configuration access** binds the class to a global, so tests cannot vary it and they interfere with each other through shared state.

**Creating its own `HttpClient`** means every test makes a real network call — slow, flaky, and dependent on something outside the test.

All three are the same problem: the class *acquires* its dependencies rather than *receiving* them, so the test has no seam to substitute at.

**The changes, smallest first**

**Time**: inject `TimeProvider` — built into .NET 8 and specifically designed for this, with `FakeTimeProvider` in the testing package for controlling it. Before .NET 8 the equivalent is a small `IClock` interface.

**Configuration**: inject `IOptions<T>` rather than reading a static. A test then constructs the options directly, with no global state and no interference between tests.

**HttpClient**: inject it, and register it through `IHttpClientFactory`. A test supplies a client with a stub message handler — which also fixes the socket exhaustion problem the original code has in production.

```csharp
// Before: acquires everything, testable only against the real world
public class ExpiryService
{
    public bool IsExpired(Order o) => o.CreatedAt < DateTime.Now.AddDays(-30);
}

// After: receives its dependencies, so a test controls them
public class ExpiryService(TimeProvider time, IOptions<Policy> policy)
{
    public bool IsExpired(Order o) =>
        o.CreatedAt < time.GetUtcNow().AddDays(-policy.Value.ExpiryDays);
}
```

**How to do it incrementally**

You do not need to rewrite everything. Each of these is an independent change that can ship separately, and each makes a slice of the class testable. Start with whichever blocks the test you actually want to write — usually the clock, because date logic is where the bugs are.

**The general principle**

Untestable usually means 'reaches out for what it needs'. Making it testable and making it correct tend to be the same change: the injected version also fixes the DST bug from using `Now` instead of `UtcNow`, and the socket exhaustion from the private `HttpClient`.

**What's happening**

An iterator method's body does not run when you call it. The compiler transforms it into a state machine; calling the method only constructs that state machine. The body — including any validation at the top — executes on the first `MoveNext`, which is when enumeration begins.

So `GetItems(null)` returns successfully and the `ArgumentNullException` appears later, possibly in a completely different part of the code, with a stack trace that points at a `foreach` rather than at the bad call.

**Why that is a problem**

The exception surfaces far from its cause, making it much harder to diagnose. Worse, if the caller never enumerates, the invalid argument is never detected at all.

**The fix: split the method**

Validate eagerly in a normal method, and delegate to a private iterator:

```csharp
public IEnumerable<Order> GetOrders(string customerId)
{
    // Runs immediately, at the call site
    ArgumentException.ThrowIfNullOrEmpty(customerId);

    return Iterate();

    IEnumerable<Order> Iterate()
    {
        foreach (var order in _source)
            if (order.CustomerId == customerId)
                yield return order;
    }
}
```

The outer method is not an iterator — it has no `yield` — so its body runs on call. The inner one carries the deferred behaviour.

**The related surprises worth knowing**

**Deferred execution means the source is read when enumerated**, not when the query is built — so a change to the source between the two is visible.

**Enumerating twice runs the body twice**, which is the multiple-enumeration problem.

**A `using` inside an iterator** is disposed when enumeration completes or the enumerator is disposed — so abandoning a `foreach` early disposes correctly, but holding the enumerable without enumerating leaves the resource unopened, which is usually fine but occasionally surprising.

**The general principle**

Laziness is a feature with a cost: the separation of when you ask from when it happens moves errors away from their cause. Validating eagerly restores the connection for the case that matters most.

**What's happening**

The request body is a forward-only, non-seekable stream by default. Once the middleware reads it to the end, the position is at the end and the model binder finds nothing to deserialise — so the controller's parameter is null or default.

It is not that the data was consumed maliciously; it is that a stream can only be read once unless you arrange otherwise.

**The fix: buffering**

`EnableBuffering()` switches the body to a seekable stream backed by memory, with a spill to disk past a threshold. Then the middleware can read it and rewind:

```csharp
app.Use(async (context, next) =>
{
    context.Request.EnableBuffering();

    using var reader = new StreamReader(
        context.Request.Body, leaveOpen: true);

    var body = await reader.ReadToEndAsync();
    context.Request.Body.Position = 0;      // rewind for the model binder

    logger.LogInformation("Request body: {Body}", body);

    await next(context);
});
```

Two details that are easy to miss: `leaveOpen: true` on the `StreamReader`, or disposing it closes the body stream; and resetting `Position` to 0, without which the model binder still finds nothing.

**What I would question about the requirement**

**Buffering has a cost.** Every request is now held in memory or spilled to disk. For large uploads that is significant, and the default threshold is only 30KB before it writes to disk — a file upload endpoint will start doing disk IO per request.

**Logging request bodies is usually a bad idea.** They contain personal data, passwords, tokens and card numbers. Once ingested into a log store it is very hard to remove, and it is a compliance problem rather than a technical one.

**What I would propose instead**

Log the metadata — path, method, status, duration, correlation id, content length — which answers most diagnostic questions. If bodies are genuinely needed, capture them only for failures, only for specific endpoints, with redaction of known sensitive fields, and with a short retention. Make it opt-in per route rather than global.

**The response body has the same problem in reverse** — reading it requires swapping the stream and copying back, which is more invasive and also worth avoiding unless necessary.

**What happened: over-posting / mass assignment**

The model binder populates every property it can match from the request body. If the model exposes `Role`, `IsAdmin` or `AccountId`, a client can set them simply by including them in the JSON — regardless of whether the UI offers that field.

If the handler then saves that object, the user has assigned themselves whatever those properties control. The API never intended to accept them; it just never said so.

**Why it is so easy to introduce**

Binding an entity directly is convenient and looks harmless:

```csharp
// Every property of User is bindable from the request body
[HttpPost]
public async Task<IActionResult> Update(User user)
{
    db.Users.Update(user);
    await db.SaveChangesAsync();
}
```

The vulnerability is created by what the model *has*, not by what the code does with it — so it appears whenever someone adds a property to the entity, long after the endpoint was reviewed.

**The fix**

**Bind to a request DTO containing only the fields the caller may set.** Then map explicitly onto the entity:

```csharp
public record UpdateProfileRequest(string DisplayName, string TimeZone);

[HttpPost]
public async Task<IActionResult> Update(UpdateProfileRequest request)
{
    var user = await db.Users.FindAsync(currentUserId);

    user.DisplayName = request.DisplayName;   // only what is permitted
    user.TimeZone = request.TimeZone;

    await db.SaveChangesAsync();
}
```

The DTO has no `Role`, so there is nothing to over-post. Notice this also fixes the authorisation problem — the entity is loaded by the *current user's* id rather than one supplied by the client.

**Why DTOs rather than `[Bind]` attributes**

`[Bind(Include = ...)]` works but is a denylist mentality applied as an allowlist afterthought — easy to forget on a new action, and invisible at the call site. A DTO makes the contract explicit and cannot silently gain a sensitive property when someone edits the entity.

**The general principle**

Never let the shape of your persistence model define your API surface. They change for different reasons, and coupling them means every new column is a potential API change and a potential vulnerability.

**Interview answer**

Singleton means one shared instance for the application lifetime. In ASP.NET Core, prefer letting the DI container manage it with `AddSingleton` rather than creating a global instance yourself. The service must be safe to share across requests and must not capture scoped dependencies such as `DbContext`.

**Registration**

```cs
builder.Services.AddSingleton<ILoggerService, LoggerService>();
```

**What to say while explaining**

`AddSingleton` tells the container to create one instance of `LoggerService` and reuse it whenever `ILoggerService` is requested, for the life of the application.

**Manual version, if the interviewer asks without DI**

`Lazy<T>` gives thread-safe initialisation with no locking code of your own; the private constructor prevents anyone creating a second instance.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public interface ILoggerService { void Log(string message); }

public class LoggerService : ILoggerService
{
    public int InstanceId { get; } = ++_created;
    private static int _created;

    public void Log(string message) => Console.WriteLine($"[logger {InstanceId}] {message}");
}

// The manual form: private constructor plus Lazy<T> for thread-safe creation.
public sealed class Logger
{
    private static readonly Lazy<Logger> _instance = new(() => new Logger());
    private static int _constructorCalls;

    private Logger() { _constructorCalls++; }

    public static Logger Instance => _instance.Value;
    public static int ConstructorCalls => _constructorCalls;

    public void Log(string message) => Console.WriteLine($"[manual] {message}");
}

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        // One shared instance, however many times it is resolved.
        ILoggerService first = new LoggerService();
        Console.WriteLine($"a second resolution would reuse instance {first.GetHashCode() == first.GetHashCode()}");

        var logger = (LoggerService)first;
        logger.Log("Order created");
        Console.WriteLine($"instances created so far: {logger.InstanceId}");

        // The manual singleton: the constructor runs at most once, even under load.
        Parallel.For(0, 50, i => { var touched = Logger.Instance; });   // from many threads

        Console.WriteLine($"manual constructor calls after 50 threads: {Logger.ConstructorCalls}");
        Console.WriteLine($"same object every time: {ReferenceEquals(Logger.Instance, Logger.Instance)}");

        // The risk: shared MUTABLE state is not made safe by the pattern.
        var counter = new SharedCounter();
        Parallel.For(0, 1000, _ => counter.UnsafeIncrement());
        Parallel.For(0, 1000, _ => counter.SafeIncrement());

        Console.WriteLine($"unsafe counter reached 1000: {counter.Unsafe == 1000}");
        Console.WriteLine($"safe counter reached 1000:   {counter.Safe == 1000}");
    }
}

public class SharedCounter
{
    private int _unsafe, _safe;
    public int Unsafe => _unsafe;
    public int Safe => _safe;

    public void UnsafeIncrement()
    {
        int read = _unsafe;
        System.Threading.Thread.Yield();   // force the interleave so the race is reproducible
        _unsafe = read + 1;
    }
    public void SafeIncrement() => System.Threading.Interlocked.Increment(ref _safe);
}
```

**Output**

```
a second resolution would reuse instance True
[logger 1] Order created
instances created so far: 1
manual constructor calls after 50 threads: 1
same object every time: True
unsafe counter reached 1000: False
safe counter reached 1000:   True
```

Note the last two lines: the singleton is created exactly once, and its mutable state is still not thread-safe.

**Key follow-ups**

- **Singleton vs static class** — a singleton is an object, can implement an interface and can be injected; a static class cannot.
- **Is it thread-safe?** Thread-safe *initialisation* does not make the state inside it thread-safe.
- **Can it inject DbContext?** Not directly — `DbContext` is scoped, so that is a captive dependency.
- **`AddSingleton` vs manual** — prefer the container, which manages creation, dependencies and disposal.

**Interview memory**

One instance → `AddSingleton` → shared across the app → watch thread safety → do not capture scoped dependencies → manual version is private constructor plus `Lazy<T>`.

**Interview answer**

Factory Method is a creational pattern that separates object creation from the code that uses the object. The client works with an abstraction while the concrete type created can vary.

**The trap to know**

A static `Create(type)` with a switch is a **Simple Factory**, not the GoF Factory Method. Factory Method uses inheritance: a base class declares the creation method and subclasses override it. If an interviewer asks specifically for Factory Method, show the overriding version.

**What to say while explaining**

Use it when creation varies, becomes complex, or the client should not depend on concrete implementations. Do not claim it should always replace `new`.

**In ASP.NET Core**, the usual real-world form is a keyed service or an injected `Func<string, INotification>`, because the container already does the resolution.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public interface INotification { void Send(string message); }

public class EmailNotification : INotification
{
    public void Send(string message) => Console.WriteLine($"Email: {message}");
}

public class SmsNotification : INotification
{
    public void Send(string message) => Console.WriteLine($"SMS: {message}");
}

// Simple Factory: centralised creation behind one method.
public static class NotificationFactory
{
    public static INotification Create(string type) => type.ToLower() switch
    {
        "email" => new EmailNotification(),
        "sms" => new SmsNotification(),
        _ => throw new ArgumentException($"Unknown type '{type}'")
    };
}

// GoF Factory Method: the base class defines the algorithm, subclasses decide the product.
public abstract class NotificationCreator
{
    public abstract INotification CreateNotification();

    public void Notify(string message)
    {
        var notification = CreateNotification();
        notification.Send(message);
    }
}

public class EmailNotificationCreator : NotificationCreator
{
    public override INotification CreateNotification() => new EmailNotification();
}

public class SmsNotificationCreator : NotificationCreator
{
    public override INotification CreateNotification() => new SmsNotification();
}

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        // Simple Factory
        NotificationFactory.Create("email").Send("Order placed");
        NotificationFactory.Create("sms").Send("Order shipped");

        try { NotificationFactory.Create("pigeon"); }
        catch (ArgumentException ex) { Console.WriteLine($"rejected: {ex.Message}"); }

        Console.WriteLine();

        // Factory Method: the caller picks a creator, not a product.
        var creators = new List<NotificationCreator>
        {
            new EmailNotificationCreator(),
            new SmsNotificationCreator()
        };

        foreach (var creator in creators) creator.Notify("Order placed");

        Console.WriteLine();

        // The DI-flavoured form: resolution by key, no switch to maintain.
        var registry = new Dictionary<string, Func<INotification>>
        {
            ["email"] = () => new EmailNotification(),
            ["sms"] = () => new SmsNotification()
        };

        registry["sms"]().Send("Resolved from the container");
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
Email: Order placed
SMS: Order shipped
rejected: Unknown type 'pigeon'

Email: Order placed
SMS: Order placed

SMS: Resolved from the container
```

**Key follow-ups**

- **Why not just `new`?** When creation varies, is complex, or the client should depend on an abstraction.
- **Factory Method vs Simple Factory** — Simple Factory centralises creation in a method or switch; Factory Method uses polymorphism so subclasses override it.
- **Factory vs Strategy** — Factory decides *which object to create*; Strategy decides *which behaviour to execute*.
- **Factory Method vs Abstract Factory** — one product via an overridable method, versus families of related products.
- **SOLID** — supports Open/Closed and Dependency Inversion by keeping the client on abstractions.

**Interview memory**

Factory Method → object creation → client uses the interface → concrete creation varies → **Factory = creation, Strategy = behaviour**.

**Interview answer**

Strategy lets you swap an algorithm or behaviour at runtime. Each algorithm is a class behind a common interface, and the context holds a reference to one of them rather than a `switch`.

The usual motivation is that a conditional over behaviour keeps growing — adding a case means editing existing, tested code. Strategy makes each behaviour a separate closed class.

**What to say while explaining**

A `switch` is not automatically wrong. Strategy earns its place when the branches are non-trivial, change independently, need their own dependencies, or must be testable in isolation. Three one-line cases in a stable method do not need a pattern.

**In ASP.NET Core**, register all implementations and resolve the one you need — the container and a dictionary replace the factory:

```cs
builder.Services.AddScoped<IDiscountStrategy, PercentageDiscount>();
builder.Services.AddScoped<IDiscountStrategy, FlatDiscount>();
// inject IEnumerable<IDiscountStrategy> and select by a property
```

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public interface IDiscountStrategy
{
    string Code { get; }
    decimal Apply(decimal amount);
}

public class NoDiscount : IDiscountStrategy
{
    public string Code => "none";
    public decimal Apply(decimal amount) => amount;
}

public class PercentageDiscount : IDiscountStrategy
{
    public string Code => "percent";
    public decimal Apply(decimal amount) => amount * 0.90m;
}

public class LoyaltyDiscount : IDiscountStrategy
{
    public string Code => "loyalty";
    public decimal Apply(decimal amount) => amount - Math.Min(amount * 0.05m, 20m);
}

// The context depends on the abstraction, never on a concrete strategy.
public class Checkout
{
    private readonly IReadOnlyDictionary<string, IDiscountStrategy> _strategies;

    public Checkout(IEnumerable<IDiscountStrategy> strategies) =>
        _strategies = strategies.ToDictionary(s => s.Code);

    public decimal Total(decimal amount, string discountCode)
    {
        var strategy = _strategies.TryGetValue(discountCode, out var found)
            ? found
            : _strategies["none"];

        return strategy.Apply(amount);
    }
}

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        // What the DI container would inject.
        var checkout = new Checkout(new IDiscountStrategy[]
        {
            new NoDiscount(), new PercentageDiscount(), new LoyaltyDiscount()
        });

        foreach (string code in new[] { "none", "percent", "loyalty", "unknown" })
            Console.WriteLine($"{code,-8} on 200.00 -> {checkout.Total(200m, code):F2}");

        // Adding a behaviour requires no change to Checkout.
        var withNew = new Checkout(new IDiscountStrategy[]
        {
            new NoDiscount(), new PercentageDiscount(), new LoyaltyDiscount(), new SeasonalDiscount()
        });

        Console.WriteLine($"seasonal on 200.00 -> {withNew.Total(200m, "seasonal"):F2}");
    }
}

public class SeasonalDiscount : IDiscountStrategy
{
    public string Code => "seasonal";
    public decimal Apply(decimal amount) => amount - 25m;
}
```

**Output**

```
none     on 200.00 -> 200.00
percent  on 200.00 -> 180.00
loyalty  on 200.00 -> 190.00
unknown  on 200.00 -> 200.00
seasonal on 200.00 -> 175.00
```

The last line is the point: a new behaviour was added and `Checkout` did not change.

**Key follow-ups**

- **Strategy vs Factory** — Factory chooses *what to create*; Strategy chooses *what to do*.
- **Strategy vs State** — the client picks a strategy; a state machine transitions itself between states.
- **Do you need classes?** In C#, a `Func<decimal, decimal>` is a lightweight strategy. Classes earn their place when the behaviour needs dependencies or its own tests.
- **Overkill when** the branches are trivial and stable — a pattern that adds four files to replace a three-line switch is a net loss.

**Interview memory**

Strategy → swap behaviour at runtime → one interface, many algorithms → context depends on the abstraction → adding a behaviour touches no existing class.

**Interview answer**

Observer lets one object notify many others when something changes, without knowing who they are. The subject keeps a list of subscribers and calls them on a change.

**How .NET supports it**

Three ways, and knowing which to use is the useful part:

- **`event`** — the built-in, idiomatic mechanism for in-process notification.
- **`IObservable<T>` / `IObserver<T>`** — the framework interfaces, and the basis of Reactive Extensions, which add composition, filtering and scheduling.
- **`IProgress<T>`** — the specialised form for progress reporting, which marshals back to the captured context.

**What to say while explaining**

In a distributed system this pattern becomes a message broker or a pub/sub topic — same idea, different failure modes, because the subscribers are no longer in the same process.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class StockTicker
{
    // The .NET-idiomatic form: an event with a typed payload.
    public event EventHandler<PriceChangedEventArgs> PriceChanged;

    private decimal _price;

    public void SetPrice(decimal price)
    {
        if (price == _price) return;

        _price = price;

        // The null-conditional call is required: no subscribers means a null delegate.
        PriceChanged?.Invoke(this, new PriceChangedEventArgs(price));
    }
}

public class PriceChangedEventArgs : EventArgs
{
    public decimal Price { get; }
    public PriceChangedEventArgs(decimal price) => Price = price;
}

public class Dashboard : IDisposable
{
    private readonly string _name;
    private readonly StockTicker _ticker;

    public Dashboard(string name, StockTicker ticker)
    {
        _name = name;
        _ticker = ticker;
        _ticker.PriceChanged += OnPriceChanged;    // subscribe
    }

    private void OnPriceChanged(object sender, PriceChangedEventArgs e) =>
        Console.WriteLine($"  {_name} saw {e.Price:F2}");

    // Without this, the ticker holds a reference to every dashboard forever.
    public void Dispose() => _ticker.PriceChanged -= OnPriceChanged;
}

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var ticker = new StockTicker();

        Console.WriteLine("no subscribers:");
        ticker.SetPrice(100m);          // the null-conditional call makes this safe

        var mobile = new Dashboard("mobile", ticker);
        var web = new Dashboard("web", ticker);

        Console.WriteLine("two subscribers:");
        ticker.SetPrice(101.5m);

        web.Dispose();

        Console.WriteLine("after one unsubscribes:");
        ticker.SetPrice(102m);

        // The pitfall: one throwing subscriber stops the rest being notified.
        var ticker2 = new StockTicker();
        ticker2.PriceChanged += (_, _) => throw new InvalidOperationException("subscriber failed");
        ticker2.PriceChanged += (_, e) => Console.WriteLine($"  second handler saw {e.Price}");

        Console.WriteLine("one subscriber throws:");
        try { ticker2.SetPrice(50m); }
        catch (InvalidOperationException) { Console.WriteLine("  the later handler never ran"); }
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
no subscribers:
two subscribers:
  mobile saw 101.50
  web saw 101.50
after one unsubscribes:
  mobile saw 102.00
one subscriber throws:
  the later handler never ran
```

**Key follow-ups**

- **The memory leak** — the subject holds a reference to every subscriber, so a short-lived subscriber that never unsubscribes is kept alive forever. This is the most common managed leak in .NET.
- **One throwing subscriber breaks the chain**, as the output shows. Wrap each invocation if isolation matters.
- **Ordering is not guaranteed** and should not be relied on.
- **Thread safety** — raising an event from multiple threads while subscriptions change needs care; copy the delegate to a local first, which `?.Invoke` does.
- **Events vs IObservable** — use events for simple in-process notification; use Rx when you need composition, filtering, throttling or backpressure.

**Interview memory**

Observer → one subject, many subscribers → `event` in .NET → **always unsubscribe** → one bad handler can break the rest → distributed version is pub/sub.

**Interview answer**

Builder constructs a complex object step by step, so the construction process is separated from the final representation. It is most useful when an object has many optional parts, or when construction has rules that a constructor cannot express cleanly.

**What to say while explaining**

C# has named and optional parameters and object initialisers, which handle many cases a builder would be used for in Java. So the honest answer is that Builder earns its place in C# when:

- construction is **validated at the end** — the object cannot exist in a partial state,
- steps have **ordering or dependency rules**,
- the same steps produce **different representations**, or
- you want a **fluent, discoverable API** for a complex configuration.

You already use builders constantly: `StringBuilder`, `WebApplicationBuilder`, `HttpRequestMessage` configuration, EF Core's `ModelBuilder`.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class Pizza
{
    public string Size { get; }
    public IReadOnlyList<string> Toppings { get; }
    public bool ExtraCheese { get; }

    // Internal so only the builder can create a valid instance.
    internal Pizza(string size, IReadOnlyList<string> toppings, bool extraCheese)
    {
        Size = size;
        Toppings = toppings;
        ExtraCheese = extraCheese;
    }

    public override string ToString() =>
        $"{Size} pizza with [{string.Join(", ", Toppings)}]{(ExtraCheese ? " + extra cheese" : "")}";
}

public class PizzaBuilder
{
    private string _size;
    private readonly List<string> _toppings = new();
    private bool _extraCheese;

    public PizzaBuilder OfSize(string size) { _size = size; return this; }

    public PizzaBuilder With(string topping)
    {
        if (_toppings.Count == 5) throw new InvalidOperationException("at most five toppings");
        _toppings.Add(topping);
        return this;
    }

    public PizzaBuilder WithExtraCheese() { _extraCheese = true; return this; }

    // Validation happens HERE, so a Pizza cannot exist in an invalid state.
    public Pizza Build()
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(_size)) throw new InvalidOperationException("size is required");
        if (_extraCheese && !_toppings.Contains("cheese"))
            throw new InvalidOperationException("extra cheese needs cheese");

        return new Pizza(_size, _toppings.ToList(), _extraCheese);
    }
}

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var pizza = new PizzaBuilder()
            .OfSize("large")
            .With("cheese")
            .With("mushroom")
            .WithExtraCheese()
            .Build();

        Console.WriteLine(pizza);

        // The rules are enforced at Build, not scattered through the caller.
        foreach (var attempt in new (string Description, Action Act)[]
        {
            ("no size", () => new PizzaBuilder().With("cheese").Build()),
            ("extra cheese without cheese", () => new PizzaBuilder().OfSize("small").WithExtraCheese().Build()),
            ("six toppings", () => { var b = new PizzaBuilder().OfSize("small");
                                      foreach (var t in Enumerable.Range(1, 6)) b.With($"t{t}"); })
        })
        {
            try { attempt.Act(); Console.WriteLine($"{attempt.Description}: allowed"); }
            catch (InvalidOperationException ex) { Console.WriteLine($"{attempt.Description}: rejected - {ex.Message}"); }
        }

        // For a simple object, C# already has a good answer - no builder needed.
        var simple = new { Size = "medium", Toppings = new[] { "ham" } };
        Console.WriteLine($"object initialiser is enough here: {simple.Size}");
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
large pizza with [cheese, mushroom] + extra cheese
no size: rejected - size is required
extra cheese without cheese: rejected - extra cheese needs cheese
six toppings: rejected - at most five toppings
object initialiser is enough here: medium
```

**Key follow-ups**

- **Builder vs optional parameters** — optional parameters are simpler and usually sufficient; Builder wins when construction must be validated as a whole or has ordering rules.
- **Builder vs object initialiser** — an initialiser cannot enforce required properties across a set, and it leaves the object briefly in a partial state. `required` members in C# 11 close much of that gap.
- **Returning `this`** gives the fluent chain, and is the part people remember — but it is the *validated `Build()`* that makes it a builder rather than a fluent setter.
- **Immutability** — the built object can be fully immutable, which is difficult with initialisers alone.

**Interview memory**

Builder → step-by-step construction → fluent chain returns `this` → **validation in `Build()`** → the product can be immutable → in C#, only worth it when initialisers cannot express the rules.

**Interview answer**

Decorator adds behaviour to an object by wrapping it in another object that implements the same interface. The caller cannot tell the difference, and the original class is not modified — which is Open/Closed in its clearest form.

**Where you already use it**

- **ASP.NET Core middleware** is a decorator chain over the request pipeline.
- **`DelegatingHandler`** in `HttpClient` — retry, logging and authentication handlers wrap the inner handler.
- **`Stream`** — `GZipStream` wrapping a `FileStream` wrapping a buffer.

**What to say while explaining**

The value is composability: caching, logging, retry and validation are each one small class, and you choose which to apply and in what order. The alternative — putting all of it in the service — gives one class doing five things.

**Registering in DI**

The built-in container has no first-class decoration, so either use a factory registration or a library such as Scrutor:

```cs
builder.Services.AddScoped<IOrderService, OrderService>();
builder.Services.Decorate<IOrderService, CachingOrderService>();   // Scrutor
```

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public interface IOrderService { string GetOrder(int id); }

public class OrderService : IOrderService
{
    public int Calls { get; private set; }

    public string GetOrder(int id)
    {
        Calls++;
        return $"order-{id}";
    }
}

// Each decorator implements the same interface and wraps an inner instance.
public class LoggingOrderService : IOrderService
{
    private readonly IOrderService _inner;
    public LoggingOrderService(IOrderService inner) => _inner = inner;

    public string GetOrder(int id)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"  -> GetOrder({id})");
        var result = _inner.GetOrder(id);
        Console.WriteLine($"  <- {result}");
        return result;
    }
}

public class CachingOrderService : IOrderService
{
    private readonly IOrderService _inner;
    private readonly Dictionary<int, string> _cache = new();

    public CachingOrderService(IOrderService inner) => _inner = inner;

    public string GetOrder(int id)
    {
        if (_cache.TryGetValue(id, out var cached)) return cached;
        return _cache[id] = _inner.GetOrder(id);
    }
}

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var core = new OrderService();

        // Order matters: caching outside logging means a cache hit logs nothing.
        IOrderService cachingOutside = new CachingOrderService(new LoggingOrderService(core));

        Console.WriteLine("caching outside logging:");
        cachingOutside.GetOrder(1);
        cachingOutside.GetOrder(1);          // served from cache, no log lines
        Console.WriteLine($"real service calls: {core.Calls}");

        var core2 = new OrderService();
        IOrderService loggingOutside = new LoggingOrderService(new CachingOrderService(core2));

        Console.WriteLine("logging outside caching:");
        loggingOutside.GetOrder(1);
        loggingOutside.GetOrder(1);          // logs both times, hits the cache once
        Console.WriteLine($"real service calls: {core2.Calls}");
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
caching outside logging:
  -> GetOrder(1)
  <- order-1
real service calls: 1
logging outside caching:
  -> GetOrder(1)
  <- order-1
  -> GetOrder(1)
  <- order-1
real service calls: 1
```

The two orderings produce different observable behaviour from the same components — which is the thing to point out.

**Key follow-ups**

- **Decorator vs Proxy** — structurally identical; Decorator *adds* behaviour, Proxy *controls access*. The intent differs, not the shape.
- **Decorator vs inheritance** — inheritance fixes the combination at compile time and multiplies classes; decorators compose at runtime.
- **Order matters**, as the output shows. Write it down.
- **Interface bloat** — every decorator must implement the whole interface, so a wide interface makes decoration painful. Another argument for Interface Segregation.

**Interview memory**

Decorator → same interface, wraps an instance → adds behaviour without changing the original → middleware and `DelegatingHandler` are decorators → **order of wrapping changes behaviour**.

**Interview answer**

Adapter makes two incompatible interfaces work together by translating one into the other. You have an interface your code expects, and a class that does the job but exposes a different shape — the adapter sits between them.

The classic real use is wrapping a third-party SDK so your domain does not depend on it directly.

**What to say while explaining**

The practical payoff is replaceability and testability: your code depends on `IPaymentGateway`, not on Stripe's client, so you can swap the provider or fake it in tests by writing one class.

**Adapter vs Facade** — an Adapter changes an interface to match what a client expects, usually one-to-one. A Facade *simplifies* a complex subsystem behind a smaller interface, usually many-to-one. Adapter is about compatibility; Facade is about simplification.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

// What our domain wants to depend on.
public interface IPaymentGateway
{
    PaymentResult Charge(decimal amount, string currency, string customerId);
}

public record PaymentResult(bool Succeeded, string Reference, string Error);

// A third-party client we do not control: different names, different shapes, cents not decimals.
public class ThirdPartyPaymentClient
{
    public ThirdPartyResponse ExecuteTransaction(long amountInMinorUnits, string isoCurrency,
                                                 string merchantCustomerRef)
    {
        if (amountInMinorUnits <= 0)
            return new ThirdPartyResponse { Status = "REJECTED", FailureCode = "INVALID_AMOUNT" };

        return new ThirdPartyResponse { Status = "OK", TransactionId = $"tx_{amountInMinorUnits}" };
    }
}

public class ThirdPartyResponse
{
    public string Status { get; set; }
    public string TransactionId { get; set; }
    public string FailureCode { get; set; }
}

// The adapter: translates our contract onto theirs, and their result back onto ours.
public class ThirdPartyPaymentAdapter : IPaymentGateway
{
    private readonly ThirdPartyPaymentClient _client;
    public ThirdPartyPaymentAdapter(ThirdPartyPaymentClient client) => _client = client;

    public PaymentResult Charge(decimal amount, string currency, string customerId)
    {
        long minorUnits = (long)Math.Round(amount * 100m);   // decimal -> cents

        var response = _client.ExecuteTransaction(minorUnits, currency.ToUpperInvariant(), customerId);

        return response.Status == "OK"
            ? new PaymentResult(true, response.TransactionId, null)
            : new PaymentResult(false, null, response.FailureCode);
    }
}

// Because the domain depends on the interface, a fake is trivial.
public class FakeGateway : IPaymentGateway
{
    public PaymentResult Charge(decimal amount, string currency, string customerId) =>
        new(true, "fake-ref", null);
}

public class Solution
{
    private static void Checkout(IPaymentGateway gateway, decimal amount)
    {
        var result = gateway.Charge(amount, "gbp", "cust-1");

        Console.WriteLine(result.Succeeded
            ? $"  charged {amount:F2} -> {result.Reference}"
            : $"  failed {amount:F2} -> {result.Error}");
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        IPaymentGateway real = new ThirdPartyPaymentAdapter(new ThirdPartyPaymentClient());

        Console.WriteLine("through the adapter:");
        Checkout(real, 19.99m);
        Checkout(real, 0m);

        Console.WriteLine("through a fake, with no SDK involved:");
        Checkout(new FakeGateway(), 19.99m);
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
through the adapter:
  charged 19.99 -> tx_1999
  failed 0.00 -> INVALID_AMOUNT
through a fake, with no SDK involved:
  charged 19.99 -> fake-ref
```

**Key follow-ups**

- **Adapter vs Facade** — compatibility versus simplification.
- **Adapter vs Decorator** — a Decorator implements the *same* interface it wraps; an Adapter implements a *different* one.
- **Where does the translation logic belong?** In the adapter, including unit conversion and error mapping — that is its job, and it is the part worth unit-testing.
- **Is it worth it for one provider?** Usually yes, for testability alone, and because 'we will only ever use one' rarely survives.

**Interview memory**

Adapter → incompatible interfaces → translate one to the other → wrap third-party SDKs → **Adapter changes shape, Facade reduces complexity**.

**Interview answer**

Facade provides one simple interface over a complex subsystem. The client calls one method; the facade coordinates the several classes needed to satisfy it.

It is not merely a wrapper — the point is *reducing* the surface a client must understand, and hiding the ordering and coordination between parts.

**What to say while explaining**

A good example is a checkout operation that must reserve stock, take payment, create an order and queue a confirmation email. The controller should call `_checkout.PlaceOrder(...)`, not orchestrate four services and know the correct order.

**When it becomes an anti-pattern**

When it turns into a god object — a class with thirty unrelated methods that everything depends on, because it became the convenient place to put anything. The facade should serve a *use case*, not be a general-purpose entry point to the whole application.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class InventoryService
{
    private readonly Dictionary<string, int> _stock = new() { ["sku-1"] = 3 };

    public bool TryReserve(string sku, int quantity)
    {
        if (!_stock.TryGetValue(sku, out int available) || available < quantity) return false;
        _stock[sku] = available - quantity;
        return true;
    }

    public void Release(string sku, int quantity) => _stock[sku] += quantity;
}

public class PaymentService
{
    public bool Charge(decimal amount) => amount <= 100m;   // pretend limit
}

public class OrderRepository
{
    public int Save(string sku, int quantity) => Random.Shared.Next(1000, 9999) * 0 + 1234;
}

public class EmailQueue
{
    public void Enqueue(string message) => Console.WriteLine($"  queued: {message}");
}

// The facade: one method, the coordination and the ordering live here.
public class CheckoutFacade
{
    private readonly InventoryService _inventory;
    private readonly PaymentService _payments;
    private readonly OrderRepository _orders;
    private readonly EmailQueue _email;

    public CheckoutFacade(InventoryService inventory, PaymentService payments,
                          OrderRepository orders, EmailQueue email)
    {
        _inventory = inventory; _payments = payments; _orders = orders; _email = email;
    }

    public string PlaceOrder(string sku, int quantity, decimal amount)
    {
        if (!_inventory.TryReserve(sku, quantity)) return "out of stock";

        if (!_payments.Charge(amount))
        {
            _inventory.Release(sku, quantity);        // the compensation the client should not own
            return "payment declined";
        }

        int orderId = _orders.Save(sku, quantity);
        _email.Enqueue($"confirmation for order {orderId}");

        return $"order {orderId} placed";
    }
}

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var checkout = new CheckoutFacade(new InventoryService(), new PaymentService(),
                                          new OrderRepository(), new EmailQueue());

        // The client makes one call and knows nothing about the four collaborators.
        Console.WriteLine(checkout.PlaceOrder("sku-1", 1, 50m));
        Console.WriteLine(checkout.PlaceOrder("sku-1", 1, 500m));   // declined, stock released
        Console.WriteLine(checkout.PlaceOrder("sku-1", 5, 50m));    // insufficient stock
        Console.WriteLine(checkout.PlaceOrder("sku-1", 2, 50m));    // the released unit is back
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
  queued: confirmation for order 1234
order 1234 placed
payment declined
out of stock
  queued: confirmation for order 1234
order 1234 placed
```

The fourth call succeeds because the failed payment released its reservation — coordination the caller never sees.

**Key follow-ups**

- **Facade vs Adapter** — Facade simplifies many into one; Adapter translates one shape into another.
- **Does it hide the subsystem?** It does not forbid direct access — clients that need the detail can still use the underlying services. That is deliberate.
- **Is it just a service class?** In practice most application services are facades. The name describes the intent, not a different construct.
- **The anti-pattern** — a facade that accumulates unrelated methods becomes a god object and a dependency magnet. Keep one facade per use case or per bounded area.

**Interview memory**

Facade → one simple entry point over several collaborators → hides coordination and ordering → **Facade simplifies, Adapter translates** → beware the god object.

**Interview answer**

Proxy controls access to another object. It implements the same interface as the real subject and stands in for it, deciding whether, when and how the real call happens.

**The variants worth naming**

- **Virtual proxy** — defers creating an expensive object until it is used. EF Core's lazy-loading proxies are exactly this.
- **Protection proxy** — enforces authorisation before delegating.
- **Remote proxy** — represents an object elsewhere; a gRPC or WCF client-side stub.
- **Caching proxy** — returns a stored result instead of calling through.

**Proxy vs Decorator**

Structurally identical: both implement the interface and hold an inner instance. The difference is **intent**, and interviewers ask this specifically.

- A **Decorator** adds behaviour and always calls through.
- A **Proxy** controls access and may *not* call through at all — that is the distinguishing behaviour.

A decorator that sometimes skips the inner call is really a proxy.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public interface IDocumentStore { string Read(string id); }

public class RealDocumentStore : IDocumentStore
{
    public int Reads { get; private set; }

    public RealDocumentStore() => Console.WriteLine("  (expensive store constructed)");

    public string Read(string id)
    {
        Reads++;
        return $"contents of {id}";
    }
}

// Virtual proxy: the real object is not created until it is actually needed.
public class LazyDocumentStore : IDocumentStore
{
    private RealDocumentStore _real;

    public string Read(string id)
    {
        _real ??= new RealDocumentStore();
        return _real.Read(id);
    }
}

// Protection proxy: may refuse, and then never calls through.
public class AuthorisingDocumentStore : IDocumentStore
{
    private readonly IDocumentStore _inner;
    private readonly Func<string, bool> _canRead;

    public AuthorisingDocumentStore(IDocumentStore inner, Func<string, bool> canRead)
    {
        _inner = inner; _canRead = canRead;
    }

    public string Read(string id) =>
        _canRead(id) ? _inner.Read(id) : throw new UnauthorizedAccessException($"denied: {id}");
}

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("virtual proxy created, nothing constructed yet:");
        IDocumentStore lazy = new LazyDocumentStore();

        Console.WriteLine("first read triggers construction:");
        Console.WriteLine($"  {lazy.Read("doc-1")}");

        Console.WriteLine("second read reuses it:");
        Console.WriteLine($"  {lazy.Read("doc-2")}");

        // Protection proxy: the denied call never reaches the real store.
        var real = new RealDocumentStore();
        IDocumentStore guarded = new AuthorisingDocumentStore(real, id => id.StartsWith("public"));

        Console.WriteLine("authorised:");
        Console.WriteLine($"  {guarded.Read("public-1")}");

        try { guarded.Read("secret-1"); }
        catch (UnauthorizedAccessException ex) { Console.WriteLine($"  {ex.Message}"); }

        Console.WriteLine($"real store was called {real.Reads} time(s) - the denial never got through");
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
virtual proxy created, nothing constructed yet:
first read triggers construction:
  (expensive store constructed)
  contents of doc-1
second read reuses it:
  contents of doc-2
  (expensive store constructed)
authorised:
  contents of public-1
  denied: secret-1
real store was called 1 time(s) - the denial never got through
```

The last line is the distinguishing evidence: a proxy can decline to delegate.

**Key follow-ups**

- **Proxy vs Decorator** — same shape, different intent; a proxy may not call through.
- **Where does .NET use it?** EF Core lazy-loading proxies, `RealProxy`/`DispatchProxy` for interception, Castle DynamicProxy behind many AOP and mocking libraries.
- **Lazy loading's cost** — the virtual proxy is exactly what causes the N+1 problem, because each navigation access triggers a query invisibly.
- **Generated at runtime** — proxies are often emitted dynamically, which is why lazy-loaded EF properties must be `virtual`.

**Interview memory**

Proxy → same interface, controls access → virtual, protection, remote, caching → **Decorator adds and always delegates; Proxy controls and may refuse**.

**Interview answer**

Template Method defines the skeleton of an algorithm in a base class and lets subclasses override individual steps without changing the overall structure. The base class owns the *order*; the subclass owns the *details*.

The key mechanic is that the template method itself should not be overridable — in C# that means non-virtual, ideally `sealed` in the base — while the steps are `abstract` or `virtual`.

**What to say while explaining**

It is inheritance-based, which is both its strength and its limitation: the structure is guaranteed, but you get one algorithm per subclass and cannot change it at runtime. Composition — Strategy or injected delegates — trades the guarantee for flexibility.

A hook method is a `virtual` step with a default implementation, so subclasses override it only if they need to.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public abstract class ReportGenerator
{
    // The template method: the ORDER is fixed and cannot be overridden.
    public string Generate()
    {
        var lines = new List<string>();

        lines.Add(Header());
        lines.AddRange(Body());

        if (IncludeFooter) lines.Add(Footer());     // a hook with a default

        return string.Join(Environment.NewLine, lines);
    }

    protected abstract string Header();             // must be supplied
    protected abstract IEnumerable<string> Body();  // must be supplied

    protected virtual bool IncludeFooter => true;   // hook: override only if needed
    protected virtual string Footer() => "-- end of report --";
}

public class SalesReport : ReportGenerator
{
    protected override string Header() => "SALES";

    protected override IEnumerable<string> Body() =>
        new[] { "north: 120", "south: 80" };
}

public class AuditReport : ReportGenerator
{
    protected override string Header() => "AUDIT";

    protected override IEnumerable<string> Body() =>
        new[] { "3 changes reviewed" };

    protected override bool IncludeFooter => false;          // uses the hook
}

// The composition alternative: same result, no inheritance, configurable at runtime.
public class ComposedReport
{
    private readonly Func<string> _header;
    private readonly Func<IEnumerable<string>> _body;

    public ComposedReport(Func<string> header, Func<IEnumerable<string>> body)
    {
        _header = header; _body = body;
    }

    public string Generate() =>
        string.Join(Environment.NewLine, new[] { _header() }.Concat(_body()));
}

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        foreach (ReportGenerator report in new ReportGenerator[] { new SalesReport(), new AuditReport() })
        {
            Console.WriteLine(report.Generate());
            Console.WriteLine("---");
        }

        // Same structure via composition - chosen at runtime rather than at compile time.
        var composed = new ComposedReport(() => "AD HOC", () => new[] { "assembled at runtime" });
        Console.WriteLine(composed.Generate());
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
SALES
north: 120
south: 80
-- end of report --
---
AUDIT
3 changes reviewed
---
AD HOC
assembled at runtime
```

**Key follow-ups**

- **Template Method vs Strategy** — Template Method uses inheritance and fixes the algorithm at compile time; Strategy uses composition and allows swapping at runtime.
- **Why should the template method be non-virtual?** Because overriding it defeats the guarantee the pattern exists to provide.
- **Hooks** — `virtual` steps with defaults let subclasses opt in rather than being forced to implement everything.
- **The inheritance cost** — one base class only, tight coupling to the base, and a change to the skeleton affects every subclass. Favour composition when the steps vary independently.
- **Where .NET uses it** — `BackgroundService.ExecuteAsync`, `Controller` action filters, `Stream`'s core read/write members.

**Interview memory**

Template Method → base class fixes the *order* → subclass fills in the *steps* → template is non-virtual, steps are abstract or virtual → **inheritance version of Strategy**.

**Interview answer**

State lets an object change its behaviour when its internal state changes, so it appears to change class. Each state is a class implementing a common interface, and the context delegates to the current one.

The distinguishing feature versus Strategy is that **states know about each other** — a state decides which state comes next, so the transition rules live with the states rather than in a central switch.

**What to say while explaining**

With an enum and a switch, adding a state means editing every switch that handles states, and there are usually several — one per operation. With State, adding one means adding one class, and the compiler tells you what you must implement.

**When the enum is fine**

Three states, two operations, rules that do not change. The pattern earns its place when the transition table is genuinely complex, when illegal transitions must be prevented, and when each state carries its own behaviour for several operations.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public interface IOrderState
{
    string Name { get; }
    IOrderState Pay();
    IOrderState Ship();
    IOrderState Cancel();
}

public class Pending : IOrderState
{
    public string Name => "Pending";
    public IOrderState Pay() => new Paid();
    public IOrderState Ship() => throw new InvalidOperationException("cannot ship before payment");
    public IOrderState Cancel() => new Cancelled();
}

public class Paid : IOrderState
{
    public string Name => "Paid";
    public IOrderState Pay() => throw new InvalidOperationException("already paid");
    public IOrderState Ship() => new Shipped();
    public IOrderState Cancel() => new Cancelled();
}

public class Shipped : IOrderState
{
    public string Name => "Shipped";
    public IOrderState Pay() => throw new InvalidOperationException("already paid");
    public IOrderState Ship() => throw new InvalidOperationException("already shipped");
    public IOrderState Cancel() => throw new InvalidOperationException("cannot cancel a shipped order");
}

public class Cancelled : IOrderState
{
    public string Name => "Cancelled";
    public IOrderState Pay() => throw new InvalidOperationException("order is cancelled");
    public IOrderState Ship() => throw new InvalidOperationException("order is cancelled");
    public IOrderState Cancel() => this;                       // idempotent
}

public class Order
{
    private IOrderState _state = new Pending();
    public string State => _state.Name;

    public void Pay() => _state = _state.Pay();
    public void Ship() => _state = _state.Ship();
    public void Cancel() => _state = _state.Cancel();
}

public class Solution
{
    private static void Try(Order order, string action, Action act)
    {
        try { act(); Console.WriteLine($"  {action,-7} -> {order.State}"); }
        catch (InvalidOperationException ex) { Console.WriteLine($"  {action,-7} -> rejected: {ex.Message}"); }
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        var happy = new Order();
        Console.WriteLine($"start: {happy.State}");
        Try(happy, "ship", happy.Ship);      // illegal from Pending
        Try(happy, "pay", happy.Pay);
        Try(happy, "ship", happy.Ship);
        Try(happy, "cancel", happy.Cancel);  // illegal from Shipped

        Console.WriteLine();

        var cancelled = new Order();
        Console.WriteLine($"start: {cancelled.State}");
        Try(cancelled, "cancel", cancelled.Cancel);
        Try(cancelled, "cancel", cancelled.Cancel);   // idempotent
        Try(cancelled, "pay", cancelled.Pay);
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
start: Pending
  ship    -> rejected: cannot ship before payment
  pay     -> Paid
  ship    -> Shipped
  cancel  -> rejected: cannot cancel a shipped order

start: Pending
  cancel  -> Cancelled
  cancel  -> Cancelled
  pay     -> rejected: order is cancelled
```

Every illegal transition is rejected by the state that owns the rule — there is no central validation to keep in sync.

**Key follow-ups**

- **State vs Strategy** — identical structure; a strategy is chosen by the client, a state transitions itself and knows its successors.
- **Why not an enum and a switch?** Adding a state means touching every switch; with State the compiler enumerates what is missing. For simple cases the enum is genuinely better.
- **Where does persistence fit?** Store the state as a value — an enum or a string — and map it back to a state object on load. Do not try to serialise the state objects.
- **Stateless states** can be singletons or cached, avoiding an allocation per transition.

**Interview memory**

State → behaviour changes with internal state → each state is a class → **states know their successors** → Strategy is chosen from outside, State transitions itself → an enum is fine until the transition rules get complicated.

**Interview answer**

Chain of Responsibility passes a request along a sequence of handlers. Each handler decides whether to handle it, pass it on, or both. The sender does not know which handler will deal with it, and handlers do not know the full chain.

**Where you already use it**

- **ASP.NET Core middleware** — each component can short-circuit by not calling `next`, or pass through and also act on the way back.
- **`DelegatingHandler`** in the `HttpClient` pipeline.
- **Exception filters and action filters** in MVC.
- **MediatR behaviours**, which is the same shape around a request handler.

**What to say while explaining**

The two useful variants are worth distinguishing: a chain where the *first* capable handler stops, such as an approval workflow, and a chain where *every* handler participates, such as logging and validation middleware. Middleware is the second kind with the option of becoming the first.

**How to decide whether to stop**

Stop when the handler has fully satisfied the request or definitively rejected it — an authentication handler returning 401 should not let the request continue. Pass through when the handler's job is cross-cutting: logging, timing, enriching context.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Request
{
    public string User { get; init; }
    public decimal Amount { get; init; }
    public List<string> Trace { get; } = new();
}

public abstract class Handler
{
    private Handler _next;

    public Handler Then(Handler next) { _next = next; return next; }

    public bool Handle(Request request)
    {
        if (!CanHandle(request))
        {
            request.Trace.Add($"{GetType().Name}: passing on");
            return _next?.Handle(request) ?? false;   // end of chain, nobody handled it
        }

        request.Trace.Add($"{GetType().Name}: handled");
        return true;
    }

    protected abstract bool CanHandle(Request request);
}

public class TeamLeadApproval : Handler
{
    protected override bool CanHandle(Request r) => r.Amount <= 100m;
}

public class ManagerApproval : Handler
{
    protected override bool CanHandle(Request r) => r.Amount <= 1000m;
}

public class DirectorApproval : Handler
{
    protected override bool CanHandle(Request r) => r.Amount <= 10000m;
}

public class Solution
{
    private static Handler BuildChain()
    {
        var lead = new TeamLeadApproval();
        lead.Then(new ManagerApproval()).Then(new DirectorApproval());
        return lead;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        foreach (decimal amount in new[] { 50m, 500m, 5000m, 50000m })
        {
            var request = new Request { User = "sam", Amount = amount };
            bool approved = BuildChain().Handle(request);

            Console.WriteLine($"{amount,7:F2} -> {(approved ? "approved" : "NOT approved")}");
            foreach (string step in request.Trace) Console.WriteLine($"          {step}");
        }
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
  50.00 -> approved
          TeamLeadApproval: handled
 500.00 -> approved
          TeamLeadApproval: passing on
          ManagerApproval: handled
5000.00 -> approved
          TeamLeadApproval: passing on
          ManagerApproval: passing on
          DirectorApproval: handled
50000.00 -> NOT approved
          TeamLeadApproval: passing on
          ManagerApproval: passing on
          DirectorApproval: passing on
```

The last case is the one to discuss: nothing handled it, and the chain must have a defined answer for that.

**Key follow-ups**

- **What if nobody handles it?** Decide explicitly — return false, throw, or add a terminal handler. Silently doing nothing is the common bug.
- **Order matters** and is part of the configuration, which is why middleware ordering is a documented concern rather than an implementation detail.
- **Chain of Responsibility vs Decorator** — a decorator always delegates and adds behaviour; a chain handler may consume the request and stop.
- **Debugging** — a long chain makes it hard to see why a request was handled where it was, which is why the trace in the example is not just for the demo.

**Interview memory**

Chain of Responsibility → handlers in sequence → each handles, passes on, or both → ASP.NET Core middleware is the canonical example → **decide what happens when nobody handles it**.

**Interview answer:** Abstract Factory gives you one object that creates a *whole family* of related products, so the products you get are guaranteed to be consistent with each other. Factory Method creates *one* product and varies it by subclassing; Abstract Factory creates *several* and varies them together.

The practical trigger is: "I have a SQL Server dialect and a PostgreSQL dialect, and the paging clause, the identifier quoting and the parameter prefix must all come from the same database." If the client picked each piece separately it could pair a SQL Server paging clause with Postgres quoting. The factory makes that combination unrepresentable.

**What to say while explaining:** point out that the client method below never names a concrete class. Adding Oracle is a new factory plus its products - no change to `BuildQuery`.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

// --- the family of products ---
public interface IQuote { string Wrap(string identifier); }
public interface IPager { string Page(int skip, int take); }
public interface IParam { string Name(string p); }

// --- SQL Server family ---
public class SqlServerQuote : IQuote { public string Wrap(string i) => "[" + i + "]"; }
public class SqlServerPager : IPager { public string Page(int s, int t) => $"OFFSET {s} ROWS FETCH NEXT {t} ROWS ONLY"; }
public class SqlServerParam : IParam { public string Name(string p) => "@" + p; }

// --- PostgreSQL family ---
public class PostgresQuote : IQuote { public string Wrap(string i) => "\"" + i + "\""; }
public class PostgresPager : IPager { public string Page(int s, int t) => $"LIMIT {t} OFFSET {s}"; }
public class PostgresParam : IParam { public string Name(string p) => ":" + p; }

// --- the abstract factory ---
public interface IDialectFactory
{
    IQuote CreateQuote();
    IPager CreatePager();
    IParam CreateParam();
}

public class SqlServerDialect : IDialectFactory
{
    public IQuote CreateQuote() => new SqlServerQuote();
    public IPager CreatePager() => new SqlServerPager();
    public IParam CreateParam() => new SqlServerParam();
}

public class PostgresDialect : IDialectFactory
{
    public IQuote CreateQuote() => new PostgresQuote();
    public IPager CreatePager() => new PostgresPager();
    public IParam CreateParam() => new PostgresParam();
}

public static class Program
{
    // Notice: not one concrete type is named in here.
    static string BuildQuery(IDialectFactory dialect)
    {
        var quote = dialect.CreateQuote();
        var pager = dialect.CreatePager();
        var param = dialect.CreateParam();

        return $"SELECT * FROM {quote.Wrap("Orders")} " +
               $"WHERE {quote.Wrap("CustomerId")} = {param.Name("customerId")} " +
               $"ORDER BY {quote.Wrap("CreatedUtc")} {pager.Page(20, 10)}";
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("SQL Server : " + BuildQuery(new SqlServerDialect()));
        Console.WriteLine("Postgres   : " + BuildQuery(new PostgresDialect()));

        // The point: you cannot accidentally mix families, because you hold one
        // factory and it hands out matching pieces.
        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("Same client method, two consistent dialects, no switch on database type.");
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
SQL Server : SELECT * FROM [Orders] WHERE [CustomerId] = @customerId ORDER BY [CreatedUtc] OFFSET 20 ROWS FETCH NEXT 10 ROWS ONLY
Postgres   : SELECT * FROM "Orders" WHERE "CustomerId" = :customerId ORDER BY "CreatedUtc" LIMIT 10 OFFSET 20

Same client method, two consistent dialects, no switch on database type.
```

**Interview memory:** Factory Method = one product, chosen by subclass. Abstract Factory = a *matching set* of products, chosen by which factory you hold.

**Interview answer:** Prototype creates a new object by copying an existing configured one instead of constructing it from scratch. You reach for it when building the object is expensive, or when the correct configuration is only known at runtime - a template document, a pre-wired test fixture, a "duplicate this order" button.

The whole interview lives in one distinction: `MemberwiseClone` is **shallow**. Value-type fields are copied, but a reference-type field copies the *reference*, so the clone and the original share the same list. Mutating the clone then changes the original, which is exactly the bug this question is testing for.

**What to say while explaining:** mention that a `record` with `with` gives you a clone for free in modern C# - and that it is still shallow, so the same trap applies.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class Address
{
    public string City { get; set; }
    public override string ToString() => City;
}

public class Order
{
    public string Reference { get; set; }
    public Address ShipTo { get; set; }
    public List<string> Lines { get; set; } = new List<string>();

    // Shallow: ShipTo and Lines stay shared with the original.
    public Order ShallowClone() => (Order)MemberwiseClone();

    // Deep: every reference the object owns is rebuilt.
    public Order DeepClone() => new Order
    {
        Reference = Reference,
        ShipTo = new Address { City = ShipTo.City },
        Lines = Lines.ToList()
    };
}

public static class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var original = new Order { Reference = "ORD-1", ShipTo = new Address { City = "Leeds" } };
        original.Lines.Add("Widget x2");

        var shallow = original.ShallowClone();
        shallow.Reference = "ORD-2";          // string field: independent
        shallow.Lines.Add("Gizmo x1");        // shared list: leaks back
        shallow.ShipTo.City = "Bristol";      // shared address: leaks back

        Console.WriteLine("-- after mutating the SHALLOW clone --");
        Console.WriteLine($"original.Reference = {original.Reference}   (unchanged: the field was copied)");
        Console.WriteLine($"original.Lines     = {string.Join(", ", original.Lines)}   (LEAKED)");
        Console.WriteLine($"original.ShipTo    = {original.ShipTo}   (LEAKED)");

        // Start again and repeat with a deep clone.
        var pristine = new Order { Reference = "ORD-1", ShipTo = new Address { City = "Leeds" } };
        pristine.Lines.Add("Widget x2");

        var deep = pristine.DeepClone();
        deep.Lines.Add("Gizmo x1");
        deep.ShipTo.City = "Bristol";

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("-- after mutating the DEEP clone --");
        Console.WriteLine($"original.Lines     = {string.Join(", ", pristine.Lines)}   (safe)");
        Console.WriteLine($"original.ShipTo    = {pristine.ShipTo}   (safe)");
        Console.WriteLine($"clone.Lines        = {string.Join(", ", deep.Lines)}");

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine($"Shallow clone shares the list?  {ReferenceEquals(original.Lines, shallow.Lines)}");
        Console.WriteLine($"Deep clone shares the list?     {ReferenceEquals(pristine.Lines, deep.Lines)}");
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
-- after mutating the SHALLOW clone --
original.Reference = ORD-1   (unchanged: the field was copied)
original.Lines     = Widget x2, Gizmo x1   (LEAKED)
original.ShipTo    = Bristol   (LEAKED)

-- after mutating the DEEP clone --
original.Lines     = Widget x2   (safe)
original.ShipTo    = Leeds   (safe)
clone.Lines        = Widget x2, Gizmo x1

Shallow clone shares the list?  True
Deep clone shares the list?     False
```

**Interview memory:** MemberwiseClone copies the *pointer*, not the thing it points at. Shallow clone = new box, same contents.

**Interview answer:** Command wraps "do this thing" in an object, with the arguments already baked in. Once the operation is an object rather than a method call, you can put it in a list - and that unlocks everything people actually want from it: undo/redo, queueing, retry, an audit trail of what was done, and running it later or on another thread.

The undo stack is why it comes up in interviews. Each command knows how to reverse itself, so `Undo` is just "pop the last command and call `Undo` on it" - no giant switch over operation types.

**What to say while explaining:** call out that the *receiver* (here `Document`) stays dumb. It knows nothing about undo. That separation is the pattern.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Text;

public class Document
{
    public StringBuilder Text { get; } = new StringBuilder();
    public override string ToString() => Text.ToString();
}

public interface ICommand
{
    string Name { get; }
    void Execute();
    void Undo();
}

public class AppendCommand : ICommand
{
    private readonly Document _doc;
    private readonly string _what;
    public AppendCommand(Document doc, string what) { _doc = doc; _what = what; }

    public string Name => "Append(\"" + _what + "\")";
    public void Execute() => _doc.Text.Append(_what);
    public void Undo() => _doc.Text.Remove(_doc.Text.Length - _what.Length, _what.Length);
}

public class UpperCaseCommand : ICommand
{
    private readonly Document _doc;
    private string _before;          // the state this command needs in order to reverse itself
    public UpperCaseCommand(Document doc) { _doc = doc; }

    public string Name => "UpperCase()";

    public void Execute()
    {
        _before = _doc.Text.ToString();
        var upper = _before.ToUpperInvariant();
        _doc.Text.Clear();
        _doc.Text.Append(upper);
    }

    public void Undo()
    {
        _doc.Text.Clear();
        _doc.Text.Append(_before);
    }
}

public class Editor
{
    private readonly Stack<ICommand> _done = new Stack<ICommand>();

    public void Run(ICommand command)
    {
        command.Execute();
        _done.Push(command);
    }

    public bool Undo()
    {
        if (_done.Count == 0) return false;      // nothing left to undo
        var command = _done.Pop();
        command.Undo();
        Console.WriteLine($"  undid {command.Name}");
        return true;
    }
}

public static class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var doc = new Document();
        var editor = new Editor();

        editor.Run(new AppendCommand(doc, "hello "));
        editor.Run(new AppendCommand(doc, "world"));
        editor.Run(new UpperCaseCommand(doc));
        Console.WriteLine($"after 3 commands : '{doc}'");

        Console.WriteLine("undoing everything:");
        while (editor.Undo())
            Console.WriteLine($"  now             : '{doc}'");

        Console.WriteLine($"undo on an empty stack returns: {editor.Undo()}");
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
after 3 commands : 'HELLO WORLD'
undoing everything:
  undid UpperCase()
  now             : 'hello world'
  undid Append("world")
  now             : 'hello '
  undid Append("hello ")
  now             : ''
undo on an empty stack returns: False
```

**Interview memory:** Command = a method call with its arguments frozen into an object, so it can be stored, replayed or reversed.

**Interview answer:** Iterator lets you walk a collection without exposing how it is stored. In C# you rarely write it by hand, because `yield return` makes the compiler generate the whole state machine - a class implementing `IEnumerator<T>` with `MoveNext` and `Current`.

The part interviewers probe is **laziness**: the body of an iterator method does not run when you call it. It runs when someone starts enumerating, and only as far as the consumer asks. That is why `First()` on an expensive iterator is cheap, and why enumerating the same sequence twice does the work twice.

**What to say while explaining:** the print statements below are the proof. They interleave with the consumer's output, which could not happen if the method had run to completion up front.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

// A hand-written iterator, so you can see what the compiler generates for you.
public class Ring<T> : IEnumerable<T>
{
    private readonly T[] _items;
    private readonly int _start;
    public Ring(T[] items, int start) { _items = items; _start = start; }

    public IEnumerator<T> GetEnumerator() => new RingEnumerator(this);
    IEnumerator IEnumerable.GetEnumerator() => GetEnumerator();

    private class RingEnumerator : IEnumerator<T>
    {
        private readonly Ring<T> _ring;
        private int _steps = -1;
        public RingEnumerator(Ring<T> ring) { _ring = ring; }

        public T Current => _ring._items[(_ring._start + _steps) % _ring._items.Length];
        object IEnumerator.Current => Current;

        public bool MoveNext() => ++_steps < _ring._items.Length;
        public void Reset() => _steps = -1;
        public void Dispose() { }
    }
}

public static class Program
{
    // The compiler turns this into a state machine. Nothing here runs on call.
    static IEnumerable<int> ExpensiveNumbers()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("    [iterator] starting");
        for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"    [iterator] producing {i}");
            yield return i;
        }
        Console.WriteLine("    [iterator] finished");
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Ring starting at index 2:");
        Console.WriteLine("  " + string.Join(", ", new Ring<string>(new[] { "A", "B", "C", "D" }, 2)));

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("Calling ExpensiveNumbers() - note there is no output yet");
        var numbers = ExpensiveNumbers();
        Console.WriteLine("  ...nothing ran.");

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("Taking just the first 2:");
        foreach (var n in numbers.Take(2))
            Console.WriteLine($"  [consumer] got {n}");
        Console.WriteLine("  the iterator never reached 3, 4, 5 or 'finished'.");

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("Enumerating the SAME sequence again re-runs the body:");
        Console.WriteLine($"  count = {numbers.Count()}");
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
Ring starting at index 2:
  C, D, A, B

Calling ExpensiveNumbers() - note there is no output yet
  ...nothing ran.

Taking just the first 2:
    [iterator] starting
    [iterator] producing 1
  [consumer] got 1
    [iterator] producing 2
  [consumer] got 2
  the iterator never reached 3, 4, 5 or 'finished'.

Enumerating the SAME sequence again re-runs the body:
    [iterator] starting
    [iterator] producing 1
    [iterator] producing 2
    [iterator] producing 3
    [iterator] producing 4
    [iterator] producing 5
    [iterator] finished
  count = 5
```

**Interview memory:** An iterator method is a recipe, not a result. Calling it cooks nothing; enumerating it cooks one course at a time.

**Interview answer:** Composite lets you treat a single object and a tree of objects through the same interface. A file and a folder both answer `Size`; the folder just answers by asking its children. The client writes one line instead of recursing itself and branching on "is this a leaf".

The payoff is that operations compose automatically. `Size`, `Print` and `Find` are each written once on the leaf and once on the composite, and they then work at any depth of nesting for free.

**What to say while explaining:** the client loop at the bottom never asks "is this a folder?". That absence is the pattern working.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public abstract class Node
{
    public string Name { get; }
    protected Node(string name) { Name = name; }

    public abstract long Size { get; }
    public abstract void Print(string indent);
}

public class FileNode : Node
{
    private readonly long _bytes;
    public FileNode(string name, long bytes) : base(name) { _bytes = bytes; }

    public override long Size => _bytes;
    public override void Print(string indent) => Console.WriteLine($"{indent}{Name} ({_bytes} bytes)");
}

public class FolderNode : Node
{
    private readonly List<Node> _children = new List<Node>();
    public FolderNode(string name) : base(name) { }

    public FolderNode Add(Node child) { _children.Add(child); return this; }

    // A folder answers by delegating to whatever it contains - files or more folders.
    public override long Size => _children.Sum(c => c.Size);

    public override void Print(string indent)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"{indent}{Name}/ ({Size} bytes total)");
        foreach (var child in _children)
            child.Print(indent + "  ");
    }
}

public static class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var tree = new FolderNode("project")
            .Add(new FileNode("README.md", 1200))
            .Add(new FolderNode("src")
                .Add(new FileNode("Program.cs", 4800))
                .Add(new FolderNode("Models")
                    .Add(new FileNode("Order.cs", 2200))
                    .Add(new FileNode("Customer.cs", 1900))))
            .Add(new FolderNode("empty-folder"));

        tree.Print("");

        Console.WriteLine();
        // The client treats a file and a folder identically - no type checks anywhere.
        var mixed = new List<Node>
        {
            new FileNode("standalone.txt", 500),
            tree
        };

        foreach (var node in mixed)
            Console.WriteLine($"{node.Name,-16} size = {node.Size}");

        Console.WriteLine($"total across the list = {mixed.Sum(n => n.Size)}");
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
project/ (10100 bytes total)
  README.md (1200 bytes)
  src/ (8900 bytes total)
    Program.cs (4800 bytes)
    Models/ (4100 bytes total)
      Order.cs (2200 bytes)
      Customer.cs (1900 bytes)
  empty-folder/ (0 bytes total)

standalone.txt   size = 500
project          size = 10100
total across the list = 10600
```

**Interview memory:** One interface, two implementations: the leaf answers directly, the branch answers by asking its children.

**Interview answer:** Flyweight is a memory optimisation. You split an object's state into **intrinsic** state - the part that repeats and can be shared - and **extrinsic** state, the part unique to each use, which is passed in at call time. The shared part is cached, so thousands of logical objects are backed by a handful of real ones.

The classic example is a text editor: a document holds a million characters but only a few dozen distinct styles. The style (font, size, weight) is intrinsic and shared; the position is extrinsic and passed to the render call.

**What to say while explaining:** be clear this is an optimisation, not an architecture. Reach for it when a profiler shows a large number of near-identical objects, not before. The instance count in the output is the evidence an interviewer wants.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

// Intrinsic state: shared and immutable. One instance per distinct style.
public class GlyphStyle
{
    public static int InstancesCreated { get; private set; }

    public string Font { get; }
    public int Size { get; }
    public bool Bold { get; }

    private GlyphStyle(string font, int size, bool bold)
    {
        Font = font; Size = size; Bold = bold;
        InstancesCreated++;
    }

    private static readonly Dictionary<string, GlyphStyle> Cache = new Dictionary<string, GlyphStyle>();

    public static GlyphStyle Get(string font, int size, bool bold)
    {
        var key = $"{font}|{size}|{bold}";
        if (!Cache.TryGetValue(key, out var style))
        {
            style = new GlyphStyle(font, size, bold);
            Cache[key] = style;
        }
        return style;
    }

    // Extrinsic state - the character and its position - arrives as arguments.
    public string Render(char c, int x, int y) => $"{c}@{x},{y} [{Font} {Size}{(Bold ? " bold" : "")}]";
}

public struct Character
{
    public char Value;
    public int X;
    public GlyphStyle Style;      // a reference to a shared object, not a copy
}

public static class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var text = "the quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog and runs away";
        var document = new List<Character>();

        for (int i = 0; i < text.Length; i++)
        {
            // The first three characters are a heading; the rest is body text.
            var style = i < 3
                ? GlyphStyle.Get("Segoe UI", 18, true)
                : GlyphStyle.Get("Segoe UI", 11, false);

            document.Add(new Character { Value = text[i], X = i * 7, Style = style });
        }

        Console.WriteLine($"characters in document  : {document.Count}");
        Console.WriteLine($"GlyphStyle objects made : {GlyphStyle.InstancesCreated}");
        Console.WriteLine();

        Console.WriteLine("first four rendered characters:");
        for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++)
            Console.WriteLine("  " + document[i].Style.Render(document[i].Value, document[i].X, 0));

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine($"do two body characters share one style object? {ReferenceEquals(document[10].Style, document[20].Style)}");
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
characters in document  : 57
GlyphStyle objects made : 2

first four rendered characters:
  t@0,0 [Segoe UI 18 bold]
  h@7,0 [Segoe UI 18 bold]
  e@14,0 [Segoe UI 18 bold]
   @21,0 [Segoe UI 11]

do two body characters share one style object? True
```

**Interview memory:** Split the state: what repeats gets cached and shared, what is unique gets passed in as an argument.

**Interview answer:** Bridge separates an abstraction from its implementation so the two can vary independently. The giveaway that you need it is a hierarchy multiplying along two dimensions at once: `PdfInvoice`, `HtmlInvoice`, `PdfStatement`, `HtmlStatement` - and now someone wants CSV, so you write two more classes.

Bridge turns that multiplication into an addition. Report types form one hierarchy, renderers another, and a report *holds* a renderer rather than inheriting from one. Adding a third renderer becomes one class instead of one per report type.

**What to say while explaining:** Bridge and Strategy look identical in code. The difference is intent - Strategy swaps an algorithm at runtime, Bridge exists to stop a hierarchy from multiplying. Say that out loud, because interviewers ask it directly.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

// --- implementation side ---
public interface IRenderer
{
    string Heading(string text);
    string Row(string label, string value);
}

public class TextRenderer : IRenderer
{
    public string Heading(string text) => "== " + text + " ==";
    public string Row(string l, string v) => $"{l,-10}: {v}";
}

public class HtmlRenderer : IRenderer
{
    public string Heading(string text) => $"<h1>{text}</h1>";
    public string Row(string l, string v) => $"<tr><td>{l}</td><td>{v}</td></tr>";
}

public class CsvRenderer : IRenderer
{
    public string Heading(string text) => "# " + text;
    public string Row(string l, string v) => l + "," + v;
}

// --- abstraction side ---
public abstract class Report
{
    protected readonly IRenderer Renderer;      // the bridge
    protected Report(IRenderer renderer) { Renderer = renderer; }
    public abstract IEnumerable<string> Build();
}

public class InvoiceReport : Report
{
    public InvoiceReport(IRenderer r) : base(r) { }
    public override IEnumerable<string> Build()
    {
        yield return Renderer.Heading("Invoice INV-1041");
        yield return Renderer.Row("Customer", "Acme Ltd");
        yield return Renderer.Row("Total", "1240.00");
    }
}

public class StatementReport : Report
{
    public StatementReport(IRenderer r) : base(r) { }
    public override IEnumerable<string> Build()
    {
        yield return Renderer.Heading("Statement Q3");
        yield return Renderer.Row("Opening", "300.00");
        yield return Renderer.Row("Closing", "1540.00");
    }
}

public static class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var renderers = new IRenderer[] { new TextRenderer(), new HtmlRenderer(), new CsvRenderer() };

        foreach (var renderer in renderers)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"--- {renderer.GetType().Name} ---");
            foreach (var line in new InvoiceReport(renderer).Build())
                Console.WriteLine(line);
            Console.WriteLine();
        }

        Console.WriteLine("--- a different report, same renderers, no new renderer classes ---");
        foreach (var line in new StatementReport(new CsvRenderer()).Build())
            Console.WriteLine(line);

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("2 reports x 3 renderers = 6 combinations from 5 classes, not 6 subclasses.");
    }
}
```

