**Interview memory:** Retry helps with a blip. A breaker helps with an outage. Without the breaker, your retries are the outage.

**Interview answer:** Retry handles transient failures - a dropped connection, a brief 503, a deadlock victim. Three things make the difference between a retry that helps and one that causes an incident:

1. **Only retry what is transient.** A 400 or a validation failure will fail identically every time; retrying it just wastes the budget. A 429 or 503 is worth retrying; a 401 is not.
2. **Back off exponentially.** Constant 100ms retries from a thousand clients is a denial of service on a recovering dependency.
3. **Add jitter.** Without it, every client that failed at the same moment retries at the same moment - the thundering herd. Jitter spreads them out, which is the whole point of the delay.

And the operation must be idempotent, or a retry after a timeout can charge a card twice. A timeout tells you that you did not get a response, not that nothing happened.

**What to say while explaining:** the demo uses a seeded random so the jitter is reproducible - real code would not seed it, but a demo that prints different numbers every run proves nothing.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class RetryPolicy
{
    private readonly int _maxAttempts;
    private readonly TimeSpan _baseDelay;
    private readonly Random _random;
    private readonly Func<Exception, bool> _isTransient;

    public RetryPolicy(int maxAttempts, TimeSpan baseDelay, Func<Exception, bool> isTransient, int seed)
    {
        _maxAttempts = maxAttempts; _baseDelay = baseDelay; _isTransient = isTransient;
        _random = new Random(seed);          // seeded ONLY so this demo is reproducible
    }

    public TimeSpan DelayFor(int attempt)
    {
        // Exponential: base * 2^(attempt-1), then full jitter across [0, that].
        var exponential = TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(_baseDelay.TotalMilliseconds * Math.Pow(2, attempt - 1));
        return TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(_random.Next(0, (int)exponential.TotalMilliseconds + 1));
    }

    public T Execute<T>(Func<int, T> action)
    {
        for (int attempt = 1; ; attempt++)
        {
            try { return action(attempt); }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                if (!_isTransient(ex))
                {
                    Console.WriteLine($"    attempt {attempt}: {ex.Message} - NOT transient, giving up immediately");
                    throw;
                }
                if (attempt >= _maxAttempts)
                {
                    Console.WriteLine($"    attempt {attempt}: {ex.Message} - out of attempts");
                    throw;
                }

                var delay = DelayFor(attempt);
                Console.WriteLine($"    attempt {attempt}: {ex.Message} - retrying in {delay.TotalMilliseconds:0}ms");
                // Real code: await Task.Delay(delay, cancellationToken);
            }
        }
    }
}

public class TransientException : Exception { public TransientException(string m) : base(m) { } }
public class PermanentException : Exception { public PermanentException(string m) : base(m) { } }

public static class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        Func<Exception, bool> isTransient = ex => ex is TransientException;

        Console.WriteLine("1. fails twice, then succeeds:");
        var policy = new RetryPolicy(5, TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(100), isTransient, seed: 42);
        var result = policy.Execute(attempt =>
        {
            if (attempt < 3) throw new TransientException("503 service unavailable");
            return "200 OK";
        });
        Console.WriteLine($"    result: {result}");

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("2. a permanent failure is not retried at all:");
        try
        {
            new RetryPolicy(5, TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(100), isTransient, seed: 42)
                .Execute<string>(attempt => throw new PermanentException("400 bad request"));
        }
        catch (PermanentException) { Console.WriteLine("    propagated after 1 attempt"); }

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("3. the backoff schedule - exponential ceiling vs jittered delay:");
        var schedule = new RetryPolicy(6, TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(100), isTransient, seed: 7);
        for (int attempt = 1; attempt <= 5; attempt++)
        {
            var ceiling = 100 * Math.Pow(2, attempt - 1);
            Console.WriteLine($"    attempt {attempt}: ceiling {ceiling,6:0}ms   jittered {schedule.DelayFor(attempt).TotalMilliseconds,6:0}ms");
        }

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("4. why jitter matters - 8 clients that all failed at the same instant:");
        var buckets = new Dictionary<string, int>();
        for (int client = 0; client < 8; client++)
        {
            var clientPolicy = new RetryPolicy(3, TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(100), isTransient, seed: client);
            // Draw the delay ONCE - calling DelayFor twice would give two different
            // random values and produce nonsensical bucket labels.
            var delayMs = clientPolicy.DelayFor(3).TotalMilliseconds;
            var floor = (int)(delayMs / 100) * 100;
            var slot = $"{floor}-{floor + 99}ms";
            buckets[slot] = buckets.TryGetValue(slot, out var n) ? n + 1 : 1;
        }
        foreach (var bucket in buckets)
            Console.WriteLine($"    {bucket.Key,-12} {new string('#', bucket.Value)} ({bucket.Value} client(s))");
        Console.WriteLine("    without jitter all 8 would land in one bucket at exactly 400ms.");
    }
}
```

