**Interview memory:** The built-in container cannot decorate. Register the concrete type, then register the interface as a factory that wraps it.

**Interview answer:** A pipeline composes independent steps around a core operation, each able to run code before and after the next step, and each able to short-circuit by not calling it.

ASP.NET Core builds this from a list of `Func<RequestDelegate, RequestDelegate>`. Each registered middleware is a function that takes "the rest of the pipeline" and returns a new delegate wrapping it. At build time the list is folded **in reverse**, so the first registered middleware ends up outermost. That reverse fold is the mechanical answer to "how does it actually work", and it is a short piece of code you can reproduce.

Two consequences worth stating: **registration order is behaviour** (authentication must precede authorization; exception handling must be near-outermost to catch anything), and **not calling `next` short-circuits** everything after it - which is exactly how an auth middleware returns 401 without the endpoint ever running.

**What to say while explaining:** the output shows the nesting directly, and shows the short-circuit case where the inner steps never print.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class Context
{
    public string User;
    public string Path;
    public int StatusCode = 200;
    public string Body;
    public override string ToString() => $"{StatusCode} {Body}";
}

public delegate void Step(Context context);

public class Pipeline
{
    private readonly List<Func<Step, Step>> _components = new List<Func<Step, Step>>();

    public Pipeline Use(Func<Step, Step> component) { _components.Add(component); return this; }

    // Convenience overload matching the shape people actually write.
    public Pipeline Use(Action<Context, Action> middleware) =>
        Use(next => context => middleware(context, () => next(context)));

    public Step Build(Step terminal)
    {
        var app = terminal;
        // Fold in REVERSE so the first registered component ends up outermost.
        foreach (var component in Enumerable.Reverse(_components))
            app = component(app);
        return app;
    }
}

public static class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var pipeline = new Pipeline()
            .Use((context, next) =>
            {
                Console.WriteLine("  > exception handler");
                try { next(); }
                catch (Exception ex)
                {
                    context.StatusCode = 500;
                    context.Body = "unhandled: " + ex.Message;
                    Console.WriteLine("  < exception handler CAUGHT it");
                    return;
                }
                Console.WriteLine("  < exception handler");
            })
            .Use((context, next) =>
            {
                Console.WriteLine("    > logging");
                next();
                Console.WriteLine($"    < logging (status {context.StatusCode})");
            })
            .Use((context, next) =>
            {
                Console.WriteLine("      > auth");
                if (context.User == null)
                {
                    context.StatusCode = 401;
                    context.Body = "unauthorized";
                    Console.WriteLine("      < auth SHORT-CIRCUITED (next was never called)");
                    return;               // nothing below this ever runs
                }
                next();
                Console.WriteLine("      < auth");
            });

        Step endpoint = context =>
        {
            Console.WriteLine("        [endpoint] running");
            if (context.Path == "/boom") throw new InvalidOperationException("database offline");
            context.Body = $"hello {context.User}";
        };

        var app = pipeline.Build(endpoint);

        Console.WriteLine("request 1: authenticated, healthy");
        var r1 = new Context { User = "sam", Path = "/orders" };
        app(r1);
        Console.WriteLine($"  => {r1}");

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("request 2: anonymous");
        var r2 = new Context { User = null, Path = "/orders" };
        app(r2);
        Console.WriteLine($"  => {r2}");

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("request 3: authenticated, endpoint throws");
        var r3 = new Context { User = "sam", Path = "/boom" };
        app(r3);
        Console.WriteLine($"  => {r3}");
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
request 1: authenticated, healthy
  > exception handler
    > logging
      > auth
        [endpoint] running
      < auth
    < logging (status 200)
  < exception handler
  => 200 hello sam

request 2: anonymous
  > exception handler
    > logging
      > auth
      < auth SHORT-CIRCUITED (next was never called)
    < logging (status 401)
  < exception handler
  => 401 unauthorized

request 3: authenticated, endpoint throws
  > exception handler
    > logging
      > auth
        [endpoint] running
  < exception handler CAUGHT it
  => 500 unhandled: database offline
```

**Interview memory:** Each middleware wraps the rest of the pipeline. Registration order is outermost-first, and skipping `next` skips everything inside you.

**Interview answer:** An ambient context makes a value available to everything in a call flow without threading it through every method signature - the current user, a correlation id, the current transaction. `HttpContext.Current` in classic ASP.NET was one; `CultureInfo.CurrentCulture` still is.

`[ThreadStatic]` cannot implement this in async code, because a continuation after `await` may resume on a different thread and the value vanishes. `AsyncLocal<T>` is the correct tool: it flows with the **execution context**, so it survives awaits and thread hops.

The trap is the direction of flow. The value flows **down** into child async operations, but a write inside a child does **not** flow back up to the parent. Each async call gets a copy-on-write view. Code that sets an `AsyncLocal` inside a task and expects the caller to see it will silently read the old value - and it will often appear to work in a synchronous test.

**What to say while explaining:** say plainly that an ambient context is hidden coupling. It is right for genuinely cross-cutting infrastructure and wrong for domain dependencies, where an explicit parameter or constructor injection is clearer and testable.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public static class CorrelationContext
{
    private static readonly AsyncLocal<string> Current = new AsyncLocal<string>();

    [ThreadStatic]
    private static string _threadStaticAttempt;    // the version that does not work

    public static string Id
    {
        get => Current.Value;
        set => Current.Value = value;
    }

    public static string ThreadStaticId
    {
        get => _threadStaticAttempt;
        set => _threadStaticAttempt = value;
    }
}

public static class Program
{
    // Returns its observation instead of printing, so concurrent children cannot
    // interleave their output and make this demo's result depend on scheduling.
    static async Task<string> DeepCall(string label)
    {
        await Task.Yield();                       // guarantees a continuation, likely on another thread
        await Task.Delay(10).ConfigureAwait(false);
        return $"  {label,-22} AsyncLocal='{CorrelationContext.Id ?? "(null)"}'";
    }

    static async Task ChildThatWrites()
    {
        CorrelationContext.Id = "written-by-child";
        await Task.Yield();
        Console.WriteLine($"  inside child           AsyncLocal='{CorrelationContext.Id}'");
    }

    static async Task HandleRequest(string requestId)
    {
        CorrelationContext.Id = requestId;
        CorrelationContext.ThreadStaticId = requestId;

        Console.WriteLine(await DeepCall("after two awaits"));

        // The value flows DOWN into parallel children. WhenAll preserves the
        // ORDER OF THE ARRAY, not the order of completion, so this is stable.
        var observations = await Task.WhenAll(
            DeepCall("parallel child A"),
            DeepCall("parallel child B"));
        foreach (var line in observations) Console.WriteLine(line);

        // ...but a write inside a child does NOT flow back UP.
        await ChildThatWrites();
        Console.WriteLine($"  back in parent         AsyncLocal='{CorrelationContext.Id}'  <- the child's write is NOT visible");
    }

    public static async Task Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("request 1:");
        await HandleRequest("req-aaa");

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("request 2 (a separate logical flow keeps its own value):");
        await HandleRequest("req-bbb");

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("[ThreadStatic] on a brand-new thread - guaranteed empty, while AsyncLocal flows:");
        CorrelationContext.Id = "req-ccc";
        CorrelationContext.ThreadStaticId = "req-ccc";
        var worker = new Thread(() =>
            Console.WriteLine($"  on a new thread        AsyncLocal='{CorrelationContext.Id ?? "(null)"}'  ThreadStatic='{CorrelationContext.ThreadStaticId ?? "(null)"}'"));
        worker.Start();
        worker.Join();

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("two concurrent requests do not see each other's value:");
        var lines = await Task.WhenAll(
            RunIsolated("req-111"),
            RunIsolated("req-222"));
        foreach (var line in lines) Console.WriteLine(line);
    }

    static async Task<string> RunIsolated(string id)
    {
        CorrelationContext.Id = id;
        await Task.Delay(20).ConfigureAwait(false);
        return $"  {id} still sees '{CorrelationContext.Id}'";
    }
}
```

