two concurrent requests do not see each other's value:
  req-111 still sees 'req-111'
  req-222 still sees 'req-222'
```

**Interview memory:** AsyncLocal flows down, never up. [ThreadStatic] does not survive an await at all.

**Interview answer:** A feature toggle decides at runtime whether a code path is active, which decouples deploying code from releasing a feature. That is the real benefit: you merge to main continuously, ship the code dark, and turn it on for 1% of users - and turn it off in seconds without a rollback.

There are a few kinds and conflating them causes most of the trouble:

- **Release toggles** - short-lived, hide unfinished work. Delete them once the feature ships.
- **Ops toggles** - kill switches for expensive or risky subsystems. Long-lived, owned by operations.
- **Experiment toggles** - A/B tests, assigned by a stable hash of the user id so a user's experience does not flicker between requests.
- **Permission toggles** - entitlements, really part of the domain rather than a toggle at all.

What goes wrong is that nobody deletes them. Toggles multiply, they interact, and with *n* toggles you nominally have 2^n configurations of which you test one or two. A toggle with no removal date becomes permanent branching in the code.

**What to say while explaining:** point at the stable-hash assignment. Random assignment per request is the most common implementation bug - the user sees the new checkout on one page and the old one on the next.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using System.Text;

public interface IFeatureToggle { bool IsEnabledFor(string userId); string Describe(); }

public class OnOffToggle : IFeatureToggle
{
    private readonly bool _on;
    public OnOffToggle(bool on) { _on = on; }
    public bool IsEnabledFor(string userId) => _on;
    public string Describe() => _on ? "on for everyone" : "off for everyone";
}

public class AllowListToggle : IFeatureToggle
{
    private readonly HashSet<string> _users;
    public AllowListToggle(params string[] users) { _users = new HashSet<string>(users); }
    public bool IsEnabledFor(string userId) => _users.Contains(userId);
    public string Describe() => "on for " + string.Join(", ", _users.OrderBy(u => u));
}

// Percentage rollout by a STABLE hash - the same user always gets the same answer.
public class PercentageToggle : IFeatureToggle
{
    private readonly string _feature;
    private readonly int _percent;
    public PercentageToggle(string feature, int percent) { _feature = feature; _percent = percent; }

    public bool IsEnabledFor(string userId)
    {
        using (var sha = SHA256.Create())
        {
            var hash = sha.ComputeHash(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(_feature + ":" + userId));
            var bucket = (hash[0] << 8 | hash[1]) % 100;      // 0..99, deterministic
            return bucket < _percent;
        }
    }
    public string Describe() => $"on for {_percent}% of users (stable per user)";
}

public class FeatureService
{
    private readonly Dictionary<string, IFeatureToggle> _toggles;
    public FeatureService(Dictionary<string, IFeatureToggle> toggles) { _toggles = toggles; }

    // An unknown feature is OFF. Failing closed is the safe default.
    public bool IsEnabled(string feature, string userId) =>
        _toggles.TryGetValue(feature, out var toggle) && toggle.IsEnabledFor(userId);

    public void Describe()
    {
        foreach (var kv in _toggles.OrderBy(k => k.Key))
            Console.WriteLine($"  {kv.Key,-18} {kv.Value.Describe()}");
    }
}

public static class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var features = new FeatureService(new Dictionary<string, IFeatureToggle>
        {
            ["new-checkout"]     = new PercentageToggle("new-checkout", 30),
            ["dark-mode"]        = new OnOffToggle(true),
            ["beta-reports"]     = new AllowListToggle("sam", "priya"),
            ["expensive-search"] = new OnOffToggle(false),      // ops kill switch, currently off
        });

        Console.WriteLine("configured toggles:");
        features.Describe();

        var users = new[] { "sam", "priya", "alex", "jo", "raj", "kim", "lee", "ana", "tom", "eve" };

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("per-user evaluation:");
        Console.WriteLine($"  {"user",-6} {"new-checkout",-13} {"beta-reports",-13} {"expensive-search"}");
        foreach (var user in users)
            Console.WriteLine($"  {user,-6} {features.IsEnabled("new-checkout", user),-13} {features.IsEnabled("beta-reports", user),-13} {features.IsEnabled("expensive-search", user)}");

        Console.WriteLine();
        var enabled = users.Count(u => features.IsEnabled("new-checkout", u));
        Console.WriteLine($"new-checkout is on for {enabled} of {users.Length} sampled users (target 30%)");

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("stability - the same user evaluated 5 times in a row:");
        Console.WriteLine("  sam  -> " + string.Join(", ", Enumerable.Range(0, 5).Select(i => features.IsEnabled("new-checkout", "sam"))));
        Console.WriteLine("  alex -> " + string.Join(", ", Enumerable.Range(0, 5).Select(i => features.IsEnabled("new-checkout", "alex"))));

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine($"an unknown feature fails closed: {features.IsEnabled("not-configured", "sam")}");
    }
}
